        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Windows 11 Is Collecting Data on Which Coding Agents and Developer Tools You Use (559 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://xusheng.dev/posts/windows-coding-agent-telemetry/</link>
            <guid>50524736</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:58:20 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://xusheng.dev/posts/windows-coding-agent-telemetry/">https://xusheng.dev/posts/windows-coding-agent-telemetry/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50524736">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><p>Reverse engineering Windows 11's MissionControlAggregator reveals coding-agent and developer-tool usage bits in a diagnostic ETW report.</p><div>
        <p>I recently asked AI to help me look through the latest Windows binaries for undocumented features and functionality. Among the leads it turned up, one list immediately caught my attention: coding agents such as <code>codex.exe</code>, <code>claude.exe</code>, <code>copilot.exe</code>, and <code>cursor.exe</code>, alongside familiar developer tools such as <code>python.exe</code>, <code>dotnet.exe</code>, <code>gcc.exe</code>, and <code>code.exe</code>.</p>
<p>Why would a Windows system DLL need a list of coding agents and developer tools?</p>
<p>The names were in <code>MissionControlAggregator.dll</code>. I asked the agent to follow the references and dig into what the DLL actually does. That led from a handful of string comparisons to a running Windows component that records which coding agents and developer tools have been used, saves that information locally, and reports it to Windows Diagnostics. And unless you have opted out of the “optional” diagnostic data (which is enabled by default), the data is uploaded to Microsoft.</p>
<p>I <a href="https://x.com/CrackmesOne/status/2122147747390468384">shared the initial finding on X</a>. This post includes more details about the investigation behind it.</p>
<h2 id="following-the-names">Following the names</h2>
<p>Opening the DLL in <a href="https://binary.ninja/">Binary Ninja</a> made the first lead easy to see. These were explicit executable names embedded in a Windows component:</p>
<p><img src="https://xusheng.dev/posts/windows-coding-agent-telemetry/agent-names.jpeg" alt="Binary Ninja showing coding-agent executable names embedded in MissionControlAggregator.dll"></p>
<p>Following the references showed that the names were used in comparisons. There are four CLI-agent slots checking <code>copilot.exe</code>, <code>claude.exe</code>, <code>codex.exe</code>, and <code>agy.exe</code>, with extensionless equivalents on the WSL route. Five desktop-agent slots check <code>github.exe</code>, <code>claude.exe</code>, <code>codex.exe</code>, <code>cursor.exe</code>, and <code>antigravity.exe</code>.</p>
<p>Here is one of the comparisons against <code>codex.exe</code>:</p>
<p><img src="https://xusheng.dev/posts/windows-coding-agent-telemetry/codex-matcher.jpeg" alt="Binary Ninja showing an exact comparison with codex.exe inside a matcher"></p>
<p>The compiler-generated C++ names are unwieldy, so I will call these functions <strong>executable-name matchers</strong>. For reference, one related matcher is <code>std::_Func_impl_no_alloc&lt;&lt;lambda_340dfe7e962ab225b6ac33bc664bd54f&gt;, bool, std::basic_string_view&lt;wchar_t, std::char_traits&lt;wchar_t&gt;&gt; const&amp;, Microsoft::Diagnostics::MissionControlAggregator::Utils::AppType&gt;::_Do_call</code>. The screenshot shows another lambda instantiation performing the same kind of name comparison.</p>
<p>The feature guarding the new agent checks has a particularly direct name: <code>CLICodingAgentUsage</code>, feature ID <code>62580080</code>.</p>
<p>And the list extends beyond AI agents. The same component checks for <code>node.exe</code>, <code>python.exe</code>, <code>dotnet.exe</code>, <code>gcc.exe</code>, <code>clang.exe</code>, <code>gdb.exe</code>, <code>javac.exe</code>, <code>devenv.exe</code>, and <code>code.exe</code>, among others. Those checks feed broader developer-tool categories. Some of these checks also verify the publisher: the Node.js slot expects <code>Node.js Foundation</code>, Python expects <code>Python Software Foundation</code>, and the .NET and Visual Studio slots expect <code>Microsoft Corporation</code>. The compiler/debugger group containing GCC and Clang has no additional publisher check in this path.</p>
<h2 id="where-does-the-dll-get-this-information">Where does the DLL get this information?</h2>
<p>The next question was whether the component scans running processes itself. Following <code>Aggregator::OnLoad</code> revealed that it subscribes to existing Windows events instead.</p>
<p>For Windows programs, it consumes <code>KernelProcess.AppStateChangeSummary</code> and <code>Win32kTraceLogging.AppInteractivitySummary</code>. These provide application identities that its handlers parse and pass to the matchers. For WSL, it consumes <code>Microsoft.Windows.Lxss.Manager.Exec</code> and <code>ExecCritical</code>, using the <code>imageName</code> field. A regular Windows <code>codex.exe</code> can therefore reach the CLI-agent checks through the app-state summary route; this is not limited to Linux programs running inside WSL.</p>
<p>The relevant flow looks like this:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="text"><span><span>Aggregator::OnLoad
</span></span><span><span>├─ WSL Exec / ExecCritical
</span></span><span><span>│  └─ ProcessWslExecEvent → CLI agent matchers
</span></span><span><span>├─ KernelProcess.AppStateChangeSummary
</span></span><span><span>│  └─ ProcessAppStateChangeSummaryEvent → CLI agent matchers
</span></span><span><span>└─ Win32kTraceLogging.AppInteractivitySummary
</span></span><span><span>   └─ ProcessAppInteractivitySummaryEvent → desktop agent matchers
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>Aggregator::OnRelativeTimerExpire
</span></span><span><span>└─ MissionControlAggregatorLogging::ReportCohorts&lt;12,4,5&gt;
</span></span><span><span>   └─ Segments.SubCohorts ETW report
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>Aggregator::OnUnload / intermediate timer
</span></span><span><span>└─ PersistentState::SaveToDisk
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A successful match sets a slot in the aggregator’s state. Later, <code>ReportCohorts&lt;12,4,5&gt;</code> turns those slots into bitsets and emits a report named <code>Segments.SubCohorts</code>. The numbers correspond to twelve broader tool categories, four CLI agents, and five desktop agents.</p>
<p><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/etw/event-tracing-portal">ETW (Event Tracing for Windows)</a> is the mechanism carrying that report. In this case, it is a self-describing TraceLogging event. Rather than writing the executable names into every report, the component assigns each category a bit. A set Codex bit means the matching tool was observed during the reporting window.</p>
<p>The source events also include runtime, time in focus, interaction counters, and resource usage. This component appears to discard those details and record only a bitmap of which agents were observed. The report’s timestamps describe the collection window, not how long each agent was used.</p>
<p>Although I found no prompt text or source code in this report, collecting which agents and developer tools someone uses already has significant privacy implications.</p>
<h2 id="is-it-actually-running-on-a-normal-machine">Is it actually running on a normal machine?</h2>
<p>Strings and code are a useful lead, but I wanted to see the component running. I checked my Parallels Windows 11 ARM64 VM, build <code>26200.9974</code>. Its <code>MissionControlAggregator.dll</code> was version <code>10.0.26100.9818</code>, and <code>CLICodingAgentUsage</code> was already enabled as a service experiment. I had not manually enabled the feature.</p>
<p>DiagTrack, the <strong>Connected User Experiences and Telemetry</strong> service, was also already running with its startup type set to <code>Automatic</code>. It starts a separate <code>AggregatorHost.exe</code> process, and that process loads the DLL. The following administrative PowerShell commands show the service, its child process, and the loaded module:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="powershell"><span><span>PS&gt; Get-Service DiagTrack | Format-Table Name,Status,StartType -AutoSize
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>Name       Status StartType
</span></span><span><span>----       ------ ---------
</span></span><span><span>DiagTrack Running Automatic
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>PS&gt; Get-Process AggregatorHost | Format-Table ProcessName,Id -AutoSize
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>ProcessName      Id
</span></span><span><span>-----------      --
</span></span><span><span>AggregatorHost <span>1500</span>
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>PS&gt; Get-CimInstance Win32_Service -Filter <span>"Name='DiagTrack'"</span> |
</span></span><span><span>       Format-List Name,State,StartMode,ProcessId
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>Name      <span>:</span> DiagTrack
</span></span><span><span>State     <span>:</span> Running
</span></span><span><span>StartMode <span>:</span> Auto
</span></span><span><span>ProcessId <span>:</span> <span>10148</span>
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>PS&gt; Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter <span>"Name='AggregatorHost.exe'"</span> |
</span></span><span><span>       Format-List ProcessId,ParentProcessId
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>ProcessId       <span>:</span> <span>1500</span>
</span></span><span><span>ParentProcessId <span>:</span> <span>10148</span>
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>PS&gt; tasklist /m MissionControlAggregator.dll
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>Image Name                     PID Modules
</span></span><span><span>========================= ======== ============================================
</span></span><span><span>AggregatorHost.exe            <span>1500</span> MissionControlAggregator.dll
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The process IDs connect the pieces: DiagTrack runs in process <code>10148</code>, which is the parent of <code>AggregatorHost.exe</code>, process <code>1500</code>; the module listing places <code>MissionControlAggregator.dll</code> inside that child.</p>
<p>The public ISO’s baseline VM had the coding-agent feature disabled by image default, so the evidence does not support saying that it is active on every Windows installation. On my ARM64 VM, however, the relevant feature and service were active before the test.</p>
<h2 id="testing-it-in-practice">Testing it in practice</h2>
<p>To test the finding in practice, I copied <code>cmd.exe</code> to a file named <code>codex.exe</code> and ran it in the interactive Windows session. The name alone was enough for the component to recognize it as Codex.</p>
<p>I captured an <code>AppStateChangeSummary</code> for the running process from the <code>KernelProcess</code> provider (<code>{2839ff94-8f12-4e1b-82e3-af7af77a450f}</code>), with <code>LaunchCount=1</code> and <code>TerminateCount=0</code>. The aggregator’s saved state contained the value <code>2</code> in CLI slot 2, the Codex slot. A separate run without an additional ETW trace also set that slot, confirming that detection worked through Windows’ existing diagnostic collection.</p>
<p>That state is persisted in:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="text"><span><span>C:\ProgramData\Microsoft\Diagnosis\AggregatorStorage\MissionControlAggregator$
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The report normally follows a roughly daily boundary. To capture it immediately, I temporarily moved the saved reporting-window start 25 hours into the past and restarted DiagTrack with a trace listening for the aggregator. The resulting <code>Segments.SubCohorts</code> event contained:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="text"><span><span>Provider: Microsoft.Windows.MissionControlAggregator
</span></span><span><span>Time:     2026-11-16T12:34:29.125116Z
</span></span><span><span>Version:  0.1.5
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>CliAgentSubCohorts:     4
</span></span><span><span>DesktopAgentSubCohorts: 0
</span></span><span><span>FlushedFromSave:       true
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The value <code>4</code> is <code>1 &lt;&lt; 2</code>: the Codex CLI bit. The name had made it through the input summary, the matcher, the persisted state, and the emitted report.</p>
<p>I also tested the developer-tool path separately, using a copy of <code>cmd.exe</code> named <code>gcc.exe</code>. It set developer-tool slot 3. With optional diagnostic data allowed, I found the resulting <code>Segments.SubCohorts</code> event in DiagTrack’s upload queue with <code>SubCohorts: [8]</code> (<code>1 &lt;&lt; 3</code>). This is the compiler/debugger category shared by GCC, Clang, and related tools, rather than a separate bit for each executable.</p>
<h2 id="following-the-report-toward-microsoft">Following the report toward Microsoft</h2>
<p>An ETW event by itself would not establish that anything leaves the machine. ETW is used for plenty of local diagnostics. The more revealing question was who was listening to this particular report.</p>
<p>The VM already had a <code>Diagtrack-Listener</code> session subscribed to both ends of the path. These PowerShell queries showed its subscriptions:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="powershell"><span><span>Get-EtwTraceProvider -SessionName Diagtrack-Listener `
</span></span><span><span>  -Guid <span>'{2839ff94-8f12-4e1b-82e3-af7af77a450f}'</span>
</span></span><span><span>
</span></span><span><span>Get-EtwTraceProvider -SessionName Diagtrack-Listener `
</span></span><span><span>  -Guid <span>'{00de11a2-ec06-58e5-1a2b-21bb21dbee4b}'</span>
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The first subscription, for <code>KernelProcess</code>, had <code>MatchAnyKeyword=0x400000000002</code>, which includes the summary’s <code>0x400000000000</code> keyword. The second, for <code>Microsoft.Windows.MissionControlAggregator</code>, had <code>MatchAnyKeyword=0xe00000000000</code>, which includes the report’s <code>0x800000000000</code> keyword. Both had level <code>255</code>.</p>
<p>The subscription showed where the report could go. To check whether Windows actually queued it for transmission, I inspected DiagTrack’s <code>EventStore.db</code> and decompressed the JSON batches in its <code>AsimovEvents</code> table. This revealed an important condition: the agent-usage report is treated as <strong>optional diagnostic data</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>When optional diagnostic data is allowed (enabled by default in the US setup I tested), Windows queues the coding-agent usage report for upload to Microsoft.</strong> I verified this by finding the agent event itself in DiagTrack’s upload queue:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="json"><span><span>{
</span></span><span><span>  <span>"name"</span>: <span>"Microsoft.Windows.MissionControlAggregator.Segments.SubCohorts"</span>,
</span></span><span><span>  <span>"ext"</span>: {
</span></span><span><span>    <span>"privacy"</span>: { <span>"isRequired"</span>: <span>false</span> }
</span></span><span><span>  },
</span></span><span><span>  <span>"data"</span>: {
</span></span><span><span>    <span>"Version"</span>: <span>"0.1.5"</span>,
</span></span><span><span>    <span>"CliAgentSubCohorts"</span>: [<span>4</span>],
</span></span><span><span>    <span>"DesktopAgentSubCohorts"</span>: [<span>0</span>],
</span></span><span><span>    <span>"FlushedFromSave"</span>: <span>true</span>
</span></span><span><span>  }
</span></span><span><span>}
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>I checked this with a clean installation of Windows 11 Home ARM64 using the latest official ISO, with the region set to the United States. Before changing any privacy settings, the <strong>Diagnostic data</strong> toggle was already enabled and labeled <strong>Include Optional</strong>. After completing setup with those defaults, <code>AllowTelemetry</code> was <code>3</code>, confirming that optional diagnostic data was enabled.</p>
<p><img src="https://xusheng.dev/posts/windows-coding-agent-telemetry/us-optional-diagnostics-default.png" alt="Windows setup with United States region: Diagnostic data defaults to Include Optional"></p>
<p>Optional diagnostic data is therefore enabled by default, at least in the US setup I tested. If you did not opt out of it during installation or disable optional diagnostic data later, it is still enabled and your coding agent usage data is being uploaded. You can decline optional diagnostics during setup or turn off <strong>Send optional diagnostic data</strong> later under <strong>Settings → Privacy &amp; security → Diagnostics &amp; feedback</strong>. Microsoft’s <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/privacy/diagnostics-feedback-and-privacy-in-windows">diagnostics and privacy guide</a> explains how to change it.</p>

      </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[How to Break into Security, Ptacek Edition (2012) (103 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://krebsonsecurity.com/2012/06/how-to-break-into-security-ptacek-edition/</link>
            <guid>50524225</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:19:23 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://krebsonsecurity.com/2012/06/how-to-break-into-security-ptacek-edition/">https://krebsonsecurity.com/2012/06/how-to-break-into-security-ptacek-edition/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50524225">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
												<p>At least once a month, sometimes more, readers write in to ask how they can break into the field of computer security. Some of the emails are from people in jobs that have nothing to do with security, but who are fascinated enough by the field to contemplate a career change. Others are already in an information technology position but are itching to segue into security. I always respond with my own set of stock answers, but each time I do this, I can’t help but feel my advice is incomplete, or at least not terribly well-rounded.</p>
<p><a href="https://krebsonsecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/howtobreakintosecurity.png"><img decoding="async" title="howtobreakintosecurity" src="https://krebsonsecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/howtobreakintosecurity-285x111.png" alt="" width="285" height="111" srcset="https://krebsonsecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/howtobreakintosecurity-285x111.png 285w, https://krebsonsecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/06/howtobreakintosecurity.png 372w" sizes="(max-width: 285px) 100vw, 285px"></a>I decided to ask some of the brightest minds in the security industry today what advice they’d give. Almost everyone I asked said they, too, frequently get asked the very same question, but each had surprisingly different takes on the subject. Today is the first installment in a series of responses to this question. When the last of the advice columns have run, I’ll create an archive of them all that will be anchored somewhere prominently on the home page. That way, the next time someone asks how they can break into security, I’ll have more to offer than just my admittedly narrow perspectives on the matter.</p>
<p>Last month, I interviewed <strong>Thomas Ptacek</strong>, founder of <a title="matasano.com" href="http://www.matasano.com/" target="_blank">Matasano Security</a>, about how companies could beef up password security in the wake of a week full of news about password leaks at <strong>LinkedIn</strong> and other online businesses. <a title="How Companies Can Beef Up Password Security" href="http://krebsonsecurity.com/2012/06/how-companies-can-beef-up-password-security/" target="_blank">Ptacek’s provocative advice</a> generated such a huge amount of reader interest and further discussion that I thought it made sense to begin this series with his thoughts:</p>
<p><strong>Ptacek:</strong> “Information security is one of the most interesting, challenging, and, if you do it carefully, rewarding fields in the technology industry. It’s one of the few technology jobs where the most fun roles are well compensated. If you grew up dreaming of developing games, the laws of supply and demand teach a harsh lesson early in your career: game development jobs are often tedious and usually pay badly. But if you watched “Sneakers” and ideated a life spent breaking or defending software, great news: infosec can be more fun in real life, and it’s fairly lucrative.<span id="more-15594"></span></p>
<p>I’m a software developer. I try to look at security through the lens of computer science. To me, the most attractive thing about the field is the opportunity it provides to work with lots of different concepts at lots of different levels. Computer security might be the best way to work professionally with compiler theory, or to spend time understanding computer microarchitecture, or to apply advanced mathematics.</p>
<p>Other people get involved in security for different reasons, and those reasons are probably equally valid. Some people really like the “good guys / bad guys” narrative that comes with security. Some people see security as an opportunity to save the world. Other people are drawn to the competitive nature of the field: at the higher levels, it really is a cat-and-mouse game. It also comes closer to meritocracy than most of technology: you can either break in or you can’t; your defenses either work or they don’t.</p>
<p>But for me, it’s just one of the best development jobs you can get, and with that in mind, here’s my advice for people interested in pursuing a career in it.</p>
<p><em>First: I want you to learn how to program.</em> Clearly, you can get through college and get a good-paying steady job without ever learning to love programming. But no one factor gives you as much control over your career, as much of an ability to write your own ticket, as the ability to solve problems using programming languages. A lot of very smart technology professionals have concluded that they just don’t enjoy writing software. Those people should reconsider. Try different languages, or different application domains, but find a way to make programming stick in your head.</p>
<p><em>Second: the best jobs in our field are in application security.</em> You can get good steady work designing firewall deployments, setting up desktop agents, or responding to incidents. But all those roles are fundamentally reacting to what’s happening in appsec. The next generation of security products — along with the org charts of security teams at the savviest companies — are being designed now, by accident, by application security practitioners.</p>
<p>Appsec roles are roles that involve attacking software or devising fixes and countermeasures for those attacks. Two good ways to “come up” in appsec: become a penetration tester, or contribute to (or start) a secure software development practice in your company.</p>
<p>A good way to move into penetration testing: grab some industry standard tools and use an <a title="aws.amazon.com/ec2/" href="http://aws.amazon.com/ec2/" target="_blank">Amazon EC2</a> account to set up a “shooting range” to attack. Some of the best-known tools are available for free: the <a title="http://www.tenable.com/products/nessus" href="http://www.tenable.com/products/nessus" target="_blank">Nessus scanner</a>, for instance, while not an application security tool, is free and can land you a network penetration testing role that you can use as a springboard to breaking applications.</p>
<p>When people reach out to Matasano asking how to get a foot in the door attacking software, we have a few simple steps to offer:</p>
<p>0. Learn to love programming in at least one language. The C Programming Language has the most cachet in application security, but for this step-by-step list, Java or Python or Ruby will do fine.</p>
<p>1. Grab a copy of&nbsp; <a title="http://www.amazon.com/The-Web-Application-Hackers-Handbook/dp/1118026470/ref=pd_sim_b_1/191-9509128-5792759" href="http://www.amazon.com/The-Web-Application-Hackers-Handbook/dp/1118026470/ref=pd_sim_b_1/191-9509128-5792759" target="_blank">The Web Application Hacker’s Handbook</a>.</p>
<p>2. Go to the “previous releases” archive at <strong>WordPress.org</strong> and grab very old versions of WordPress; install them at EC2.</p>
<p>3. Download <a title="https://www.owasp.org/index.php/WebScarab_Getting_Started" href="https://www.owasp.org/index.php/WebScarab_Getting_Started" target="_blank">OWASP WebScarab</a> or <a title="http://portswigger.net/burp/download.html" href="http://portswigger.net/burp/download.html" target="_blank">Burp Suite Free Edition</a>, both of which are free, and use them to find bugs in ancient WordPress.</p>
<p>If you’re already in an IT role, and want to come up on the defensive side of appsec, try to position yourself near custom software development. Most large firms build “line of business” applications. As a rule, building “line of business” isn’t particularly fun. But defending those apps can be; sometimes, the most boring applications turn out to be surprisingly sensitive.</p>
<p>And the good news for doing appsec in BigCo’s: most companies have very immature security programs. If you can get a role in QA, or in what the cool kids are calling “DevOps”, you can end up with a lot of influence in security. At Matasano, we’ve watched lots of ops people successfully transition to appsec and senior security management just by instituting basic security testing processes on their company’s software.</p>
<p>To me, the whole field boils down to studying, understanding, and manipulating technology. Like most software security practices, we’ve gotten our hands dirty with a fascinating cross-section of the whole technology industry. We’ve written attack tools that target the control registers of device chipsets, we’ve done custom RF work, we’ve written low-level debuggers for different CPU architectures, and we’ve seen and beaten up on products built in almost every programming language and every platform you can imagine. If crawling through the ventilation ducts of the world’s most important technology is something you think might make you tick like we do… well, appsec! Learn to code. Spend a couple days Starbucks money on an EC2 account and deploy some broken apps to break. Find opportunities to practice in your job.</p>
<p>And, uh, come talk to us? People like you are hard to find!”</p>
											</div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[United Airlines experiencing a widespread system outage, airline says (235 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/16/us/united-airlines-outage</link>
