            <title><![CDATA[Grazing sheep in fields with solar arrays (258 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://news.cornell.edu/stories/2026/10/sheep-grazing-solar-fields-offers-farmers-economic-ecological-gains</link>
            <guid>50520182</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:27:46 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://news.cornell.edu/stories/2026/10/sheep-grazing-solar-fields-offers-farmers-economic-ecological-gains">https://news.cornell.edu/stories/2026/10/sheep-grazing-solar-fields-offers-farmers-economic-ecological-gains</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50520182">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
<p><span>Two new research papers shed light on the growing practice of grazing sheep in fields with solar arrays. One demonstrates that, in the right numbers, sheep grazing increases populations of beneficial insects like bees and lady beetles compared to mowing around solar panels. The other research describes a decision-making support tool to help farmers profitably grow their solar grazing herds.</span></p>
<p><a href="http://ny.cornell.edu/"><img alt="Cornell impacting New York State" src="https://media.univcomm.cornell.edu/chronicle/initiatives/nysimpact/nys-impact.png" width="200"></a></p>
<p><span>A team from the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) found that when two sheep per hectare graze around solar arrays, the abundance of wild bees, honey bees and lady beetles jumped by 38%, compared to a control plot that was mowed. The increase was driven primarily by an 83% increase in honey bees alone, according to the team’s findings in “</span><a href="https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aeb145"><span>Low solar-grazing densities support beneficial insects in agrivoltaic systems</span></a><span>,” published Oct. 7 in Environmental Research Letters.</span></p>
<p><span>“Sheep grazing and beneficial insect habitat are two prominent conversation topics around solar development right now,” said Leana Zoller, the paper’s first author and a postdoctoral associate in natural resources in the Cornell CALS Ashley School of Global Development and the Environment. “But what’s been missing is research that treats them as parts of the same system. When we talk about sustainable solar development, integrating these discussions into a single framework is really helpful to farmers and community decision makers.”</span></p>
<p><span>For three years, Zoller and a team of entomologists, ecologists and animal and plant scientists tested various stocking rates – how many sheep per hectare are allowed to graze a certain plot of land – using Cornell’s teaching sheep grazing at the university-owned&nbsp;</span><a href="https://sustainable.cornell.edu/news/cascadilla-community-solar-farm-cornell-university-connects-nyseg-grid"><span>Cascadilla Community Solar Farm</span></a><span> in Dryden, New York. At four sheep per hectare, they found beneficial insect abundance was similar to the mowed control site, while vegetation management effectiveness was greater than at two sheep per hectare. At eight sheep per hectare, vegetation management was maximized and sheep flock health remained high, but honey bee abundance dropped by 45%.</span></p>
<p><span>“We zeroed in on stocking rates because that’s a concrete number that sheep farmers understand and work with every day, and we want this research to be useful,” said&nbsp;</span><a href="https://cals.cornell.edu/people/steve-grodsky"><span>Steve Grodsky</span></a><span>, paper senior author and assistant professor of natural resources in the Ashley School. “With solar grazing, people, energy, agricultural animals, plants and insects are all part of this ecosystem. We’re trying to understand the connections between those pieces so that when solar developers, municipalities looking at solar development, or farmers are faced with a decision, they can have scientific information about potential trade-offs and synergies.”</span></p>
<p><span>Grodsky is also assistant unit leader of the U.S. Geological Survey New York Cooperative Fish and Wildlife Research Unit. Additional co-authors are sheep farmer and solar grazing consultant Nikola Kochendoerfer, Ph.D. ’20;&nbsp;</span><a href="https://cals.cornell.edu/people/antonio-toni-ditommaso"><span>Antonio DiTommaso</span></a><span>, professor in the School of Integrative Plant Science;</span><a href="https://cals.cornell.edu/people/john-e-losey"><span> John Losey</span></a><span>, professor of entomology;&nbsp;</span><a href="https://cals.cornell.edu/people/scott-mcart"><span>Scott McArt</span></a><span>, Ph.D. ’12, associate professor of entomology; Paige Muñiz, formerly a technician in McArt’s lab; and&nbsp;</span><a href="https://cals.cornell.edu/people/todd-ugine"><span>Todd Ugine</span></a><span>, Ph.D. ’04, research associate in Losey’s lab, all in CALS.&nbsp;</span><a href="https://sustainable.cornell.edu/about/our-team"><span>Sarah Carson</span></a><span>, director of Cornell’s Campus Sustainability Office, was key in coordinating site access between researchers and the solar company that manages the Cascadilla solar farm.</span></p>
<p><span>Another Cornell research team, from the&nbsp;Cornell SC Johnson College of Business, has developed a tool that helps farmers whose sheep graze in solar arrays to make better decisions about their operation. The team described the tool in “</span><a href="https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/msom.2025.0305"><span>Flock Management for Solar Grazing</span></a><span>,” which published Sept. 28 in Manufacturing &amp; Service Operations Management.</span></p>
<p><span>After a chance encounter with Caleb Scott, Ithaca-based sheep farmer and founder of&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.unitedagrivoltaics.com/"><span>United Agrivoltaics</span></a><span>,&nbsp;</span><a href="https://business.cornell.edu/profiles/vws8/"><span>Vincent Slaugh</span></a><span>, assistant professor at the Cornell Peter and Stephanie Nolan School of Hotel Administration and first author of the paper, began thinking through the economics and logistics of solar grazing. He recruited&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.duffield.cornell.edu/people/linwei-xin/"><span>Linwei Xin</span></a><span>, associate professor of operations research and information engineering, and&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.duffield.cornell.edu/people/shane-g-henderson/"><span>Shane Henderson</span></a><span>, Charles W. Lake, Jr. Professor in Productivity, both in the Cornell Duffield College of Engineering, to work on the topic.</span></p>
<p><span>Together, they developed a spreadsheet-based model that helps sheep farmers to optimize procurement and sales decisions, based on factors like target flock size, costs, anticipated revenues, and expected vegetation in particular solar sites; if a farmer doesn’t have enough sheep to keep the grass down, they’ll have to cut it some other way. It also helps solar developers understand growers’ needs and develop contracts that are more profitable and sustainable for all parties long-term. United Agrivoltaics has already incorporated the tool into its onboarding process.</span></p>
<p><span>Slaugh sees opportunity for U.S. shepherds to grow their operations – especially with solar contracts offering a steady source of income and animal feed. Current domestic sheep production for meat and wool doesn’t come close to meeting domestic demand; about&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1550742418302392"><span>half of all lamb and mutton consumed in the U.S. is imported</span></a><span>, primarily from New Zealand and Australia.</span></p>
<p><span>“If we grow the sheep industry, will the American diet eventually include more lamb? Could that be a side effect of the solar energy revolution? I think the answer is very likely yes,” Slaugh said. “And then when that happens, we’d expect processors and supporting industries to come back, for banks to be willing to give more loans, and for supply chains to follow. All of that grows the economy, and rural economies in particular.”</span></p>
<p><span>Kochendoerfer, who grazes 700 ewes on solar installations in New York and Vermont, said that economy is already growing.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span>“I work with a lot of farms that are now scaling up because of solar,” Kochendoerfer said. “There is a new wave of sheep farmers who are capitalizing on this access to good land. With solar grazing, you get paid to graze as opposed to having to pay to graze. That leads to sustainable, meaningful economic income that these farmers can use to expand, build a barn, buy equipment or hire someone. It’s enabling a lot of folks to build sound, viable agricultural businesses.”</span></p>
<p><span>Funding for these research projects came from the Cornell Atkinson Center for Sustainability, the National Institute for Food and Agriculture and the National Science Foundation.</span></p>
</div><p><em><span>Krisy Gashler is a writer for the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences.</span></em></p></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Jiff 1.0: A datetime library for Rust (361 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://burntsushi.net/jiff/</link>
            <guid>50519744</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:53:39 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://burntsushi.net/jiff/">https://burntsushi.net/jiff/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50519744">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div role="main">
      

      <article>
      <p><a href="https://github.com/BurntSushi/jiff">Jiff</a> is a datetime library for Rust that encourages you to jump into the
<a href="https://blog.codinghorror.com/falling-into-the-pit-of-success/">pit of success</a>. Jiff automatically handles many common problems related to
daylight saving time by putting time zone handling front and center into the
API. Jiff has already seen widespread <a href="https://crates.io/crates/jiff/reverse_dependencies">adoption</a> in the Rust ecosystem.</p>
<p>Jiff is pronounced like “gif” with a soft “g,” as in “gem.”</p>
<p>This blog announces the Jiff 1.0 release by giving a tour of its API.</p>
<p><strong>Target audience</strong>: Rust programmers who need convenient datetime handling.</p>
<h2 id="table-of-contents">Table of Contents</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="#the-jiff-10-commitment">The Jiff 1.0 Commitment</a></li>
<li><a href="#introduction">Introduction</a>
<ul>
<li><a href="#follow-along">Follow Along</a></li>
<li><a href="#terms">Terms</a></li>
<li><a href="#the-datetime-onion">The DateTime Onion</a></li>
<li><a href="#defining-a-time-zone">Defining a time zone</a></li>
<li><a href="#durations">Durations</a></li>
<li><a href="#when-should-i-use-jiff">When should I use Jiff?</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#parsing-and-formatting">Parsing and Formatting</a>
<ul>
<li><a href="#fromstr-and-display"><code>FromStr</code> and <code>Display</code></a></li>
<li><a href="#the-friendly-duration-format">The “friendly” Duration Format</a></li>
<li><a href="#strptime-and-strftime"><code>strptime</code> and <code>strftime</code></a></li>
<li><a href="#the-jifffmt-module">The <code>jiff::fmt</code> module</a></li>
<li><a href="#locale-sensitive-formatting-via-icu4x-integration">Locale Sensitive Formatting via ICU4X Integration</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#date-manipulation">Date Manipulation</a>
<ul>
<li><a href="#date-basics">Date Basics</a></li>
<li><a href="#date-arithmetic">Date Arithmetic</a></li>
<li><a href="#date-rounding">Date Rounding</a></li>
<li><a href="#other-calendars">Other Calendars</a></li>
<li><a href="#which-datetime-type-do-i-use">Which DateTime Type Do I Use?</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#span-manipulation">Span Manipulation</a></li>
<li><a href="#conclusion">Conclusion</a>
<ul>
<li><a href="#motivation">Motivation</a></li>
<li><a href="#inspiration">Inspiration</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="the-jiff-10-commitment">The Jiff 1.0 Commitment</h2>
<p>Jiff is already used in production in many projects: uv, Cargo, Starship, fd,
Nushell, mise, Jujutsu, uutils/coreutils and even as the basis for a Temporal
implementation in AWS LLRT. It is time to call it 1.0.</p>
<p>Unless some significant defect is found in Jiff’s API, my plan is to commit
to Jiff 1.0 indefinitely. This is important because the intent is for Jiff
to be an ecosystem building block that appears in the public API of other
libraries. This makes Jiff an attractive <em>public</em> dependency, which means
semver incompatible releases are likely to cause significant pain in the
ecosystem. They will not be made lightly.</p>
<p>To see a list of breaking changes between Jiff 0.2 and Jiff 1.0, please
consult the <a href="https://github.com/BurntSushi/jiff/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md#100-2026-11-15">CHANGELOG</a>.</p>
<h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
<p>In programming, a datetime library’s core functionality rests at the
intersection of a computer system’s concept of time and a human’s concept of
time. The prevailing notion of system time is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_time">Unix time</a>. A human’s concept
of time is far more complex, but the most popular such model is the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorian_calendar">Gregorian
calendar</a> coupled with either a 12-hour or 24-hour clock. At the core of every
datetime library rests some routine for converting between these concepts.</p>
<p>A datetime library typically provides much more than this. Indeed, datetime
handling has <a href="https://infiniteundo.com/post/25326999628/falsehoods-programmers-believe-about-time">historically been very difficult</a>. My own belief is
that the difficulty comes from <em>poor modeling of the domain</em>. Instead of
encapsulating the complexity inside a reusable bundle of code, many datetime
libraries pass some or all of the complexity on to its users. Jiff instead
tries hard to encapsulate the complexity and make it difficult for users to
do something wrong. (<a href="#inspiration">Jiff is not the first to do this!</a>)</p>
<p>In this section of the blog, we’ll do some very basic introductions.</p>
<h3 id="follow-along">Follow Along</h3>
<p>To follow along with the examples in this blog, create a new Cargo project
with dependencies on <code>jiff</code> and <code>anyhow</code>:</p>
<pre tabindex="0"><code>mkdir jiff-playground
cd jiff-playground
cargo init --bin
cargo add jiff anyhow
$EDITOR src/main.rs
</code></pre><p>Most examples are a complete self-contained Rust program. Just copy the code
and replace whatever is in <code>src/main.rs</code>. Then run the program:</p>
<pre tabindex="0"><code>cargo run
</code></pre><h3 id="terms">Terms</h3>
<p>I am loath to present a dictionary. However, the datetime domain is littered
with confusable terms, so I think it’s important to orient ourselves on a
shared vocabulary. I’ve kept this section as small as possible. (Some terms,
like “time zone,” are omitted here because they are best defined through
exploration in the sections that follow.)</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_time">Civil time</a>: The time you see on your clock. And in general, the time that
the humans in your approximate geographic vicinity also see. That is, civil
time is a human coordinated agreement for communicating time in a particular
geographic region. Civil time is also known as: local time, plain time, naive
time, clock time and others.</li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coordinated_Universal_Time">UTC</a>: An abbreviation for <em>Coordinated Universal Time</em>; it is a time
standard that serves as the basis for civil time around the world. It was
originally intended to roughly correspond to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_time#Mean_solar_time">mean solar time</a>.</li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Atomic_Time">TAI</a>: An abbreviation for <em>International Atomic Time</em>; it is a time standard
as measured by atomic clocks. It isn’t directly connected to mean solar time.</li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leap_second">Leap second</a>: An adjustment to TAI to bring it closer to mean solar time
and thus forming the basis of UTC.</li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_time">Unix time</a>: UTC, but ignoring leap seconds.</li>
</ul>
<p>TAI and leap seconds won’t be a feature of this blog, but they are listed here
in order to convey the difference between Unix time and UTC.</p>
<h3 id="the-datetime-onion">The DateTime Onion</h3>
<p>As a way to transition into some of the core types of Jiff, I’ve come to think
of its datetime types like an onion. In reverse order, we can build up
its layers all the way to a <code>ZonedDateTime</code>. So let’s start with more terms,
but this time, they are also types in Jiff:</p>
<ul>
<li>A <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.Date.html"><code>civil::Date</code></a> is a plain Gregorian date, unattached to any specific
location.</li>
<li>A <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.Time.html"><code>civil::Time</code></a> is a plain time on a clock, to nanosecond precision,
unattached to any specific location.</li>
<li>A <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.DateTime.html"><code>civil::DateTime</code></a> combines a <code>civil::Date</code> and a <code>civil::Time</code> to provide
both a Gregorian date and a time on a clock. It is also unattached to any
specific location.</li>
<li>A <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Timestamp.html"><code>Timestamp</code></a> is an unambiguous specific instant in time regardless of
geographic location, represented as the number of nanoseconds since the Unix
epoch.</li>
<li>A <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/tz/struct.TimeZone.html"><code>TimeZone</code></a> is a set of rules for mapping a <code>Timestamp</code> to a
<code>civil::DateTime</code>, and vice versa, in a specific geographic region.</li>
<li>A <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTime.html"><code>ZonedDateTime</code></a> combines a <code>Timestamp</code>, a <code>civil::DateTime</code> and a
<code>TimeZone</code> to represent a specific instant in time in a specific geographic
location.</li>
</ul>
<p>To make this a bit more concrete, we can see what this looks like in code.
Since a time zone can map from a civil datetime to an instant in time, one way
to create a zoned datetime requires only a civil datetime and a time zone:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>civil</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>time</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>time</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>datetime</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>.</span><span>to_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>time</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// zdt is an initialism for "ZonedDateTime"
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>datetime</span><span>.</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>timestamp</span><span>().</span><span>as_second</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>1793572200</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Another way to create a zoned datetime requires only a timestamp and a time
zone. This is because time zones can <em>also</em> map a timestamp to a particular
civil datetime:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Timestamp</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>timestamp</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Timestamp</span>::<span>from_second</span><span>(</span><span>1793572200</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>timestamp</span><span>.</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// This is a shortcut for creating a civil datetime.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// The previous example spells it out a bit more verbosely
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// for clarity.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>expected</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>datetime</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>expected</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Notice how we get the equivalent zoned datetime in both cases, but we come at
it from different directions. In the first case, we start with a time that we
might see on our clocks and calendars. In the second case, we start with a
particular instant in time that we might get from a database or file system or
some other system call.</p>
<h3 id="defining-a-time-zone">Defining a time zone</h3>
<p>There is one piece missing from the zoned datetime onion examples: the
time zone <em>offset</em>. An offset represents the amount of time one must
adjust UTC time in order to get civil time. For example, consider the time
<code>2026-11-01T22:30:00</code> in UTC. In order to get the civil datetime for someone
living in New York, one would subtract 5 hours:</p>
<pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="ignore">2026-11-01T22:30:00 - 05:00 = 2026-11-01T17:30:00
</code></pre><p>Indeed, every zoned datetime has an offset associated with it:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Timestamp</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>timestamp</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Timestamp</span>::<span>from_second</span><span>(</span><span>1793572200</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>timestamp</span><span>.</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>().</span><span>seconds</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span>(</span><span>5</span><span> </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>60</span><span> </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>60</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>dt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>dt</span><span>.</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>tz</span>::<span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>5</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><blockquote>
<p><strong>NOTE</strong>: The <code>America/New_York</code> string you see in the above example is an
IANA Time Zone Database Identifier. The <a href="https://www.iana.org/time-zones">IANA Time Zone Database</a> is a
collection of time zone definitions that can be referenced by a short
identifier string. Examples: <code>Australia/Sydney</code>, <code>Asia/Tokyo</code>,
<code>Europe/Berlin</code>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>When a civil datetime is combined with an offset, then what you get is always
an instant in time.</p>
<p>With the concept of an offset introduced, we can now give a more precise
definition of a time zone. A time zone <strong>is a geographic region in which civil
clocks agree</strong> and which provides the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>A mapping from an instant in time to an offset from UTC. The offset applied
to an instant yields a civil datetime.</li>
<li>A mapping from a civil datetime to a possibly ambiguous offset from UTC.
The offset applied to a civil datetime yields an instant.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both mappings have been demonstrated in prior examples, but the mapping
from civil datetime to an offset seems to be rather complicated due to some
potential ambiguity. When does that ambiguity arise? An ambiguous offset occurs
when the civil datetime either doesn’t correspond to a valid clock time in this
time zone (e.g., when clocks are moved ahead for <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daylight_saving_time">daylight saving time</a>) or
where there could be multiple valid clock times (e.g., when clocks are moved
backward for <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daylight_saving_time">daylight saving time</a>).</p>
<p>Let’s look at some examples. In this first example, we’ll take a look at times
around the daylight saving time transition from summer time to standard time
in New York. In New York, the clocks were “set back” by an hour at exactly 2AM.
This caused the 1AM hour to repeat itself.</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>tz</span>::<span>{</span><span>offset</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>TimeZone</span><span>},</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>TimeZone</span>::<span>get</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// During summer time, New York's offset from UTC is -04:00.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>to_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>clone</span><span>())</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>4</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// During standard time, New York's offset from UTC is -05:00.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>to_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>clone</span><span>())</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>5</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// But what does 01:30AM mean? Is it the first repetition or the second?
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Jiff automatically chooses the first by default.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>to_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>clone</span><span>())</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>4</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>The above example shows how Jiff resolves the ambiguity when a “fold” occurs,
or when there is a time zone transition that moves the civil time backwards.
But we can actually dig down into lower level APIs to change how that ambiguity
is resolved. By default, Jiff uses a “compatible” strategy that always selects
the first repetition of a time in a fold. But other strategies can be selected
by using APIs on a <code>TimeZone</code> directly:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>tz</span>::<span>{</span><span>offset</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>TimeZone</span><span>},</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>TimeZone</span>::<span>get</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>dt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// The same that Jiff uses by default.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>).</span><span>compatible</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>4</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Always selects the earlier datetime.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// For a fold, this is the same as the
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// default "compatible" strategy.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>).</span><span>earlier</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>4</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Always selects the later datetime.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// For a fold, this selects the second
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// occurrence of the 1AM hour!
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>).</span><span>later</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>5</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Always returns an error when there
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// is any ambiguity.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>unambiguous</span><span>()</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>unwrap_err</span><span>()</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"error converting datetime to instant in time zone America/New_York: </span><span>\</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>         datetime is ambiguous </span><span>\</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>         since it falls into a fold between offsets -04 and -05"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>The same idea applies to <em>gaps</em>, or time zone transitions where there’s some
civil time that gets skipped on civil clocks. For example, on March 8, 2026
at 2AM, civil time skipped ahead to 3AM:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>tz</span>::<span>{</span><span>offset</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>TimeZone</span><span>},</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>TimeZone</span>::<span>get</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>dt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>8</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// The "compatible" strategy selects
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// the later time in the case of gaps.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>).</span><span>compatible</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>4</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Always selects the earlier datetime.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// For a gap, this selects the hour
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// before the gap.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>).</span><span>earlier</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>5</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Always selects the later datetime.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// For a gap, this is the same as the
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// default "compatible" strategy.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>).</span><span>later</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>offset</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>4</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Always returns an error when there
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// is any ambiguity.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>tz</span><span>.</span><span>to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>unambiguous</span><span>()</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>unwrap_err</span><span>()</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"error converting datetime to instant in time zone America/New_York: </span><span>\</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>         datetime is ambiguous </span><span>\</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>         since it falls into a gap between offsets -05 and -04"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="durations">Durations</h3>
<p>There are two remaining core types, both representing some notion of a
duration:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Span.html"><code>Span</code></a> is a powerful hybrid duration that combines both calendar units
(years, months, weeks, days) and physical time units (hours, minutes, seconds,
milliseconds, microseconds, nanoseconds). It is a beefy type that preserves the
individual unit values.</li>
<li><a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.SignedDuration.html"><code>SignedDuration</code></a> is a signed version of <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/time/struct.Duration.html"><code>std::time::Duration</code></a> and always
corresponds to a 96-bit integer number of nanoseconds. It can only represent
physical time (units of hours or smaller) and not calendar units (units of days
or greater).</li>
</ul>
<p>A <code>Span</code> is the preferred duration type to use whenever possible. Here’s a
simple example that shows how to get the number of days between two civil
dates:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>d2</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>get_days</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>29</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// or, equivalently:
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d1</span><span>.</span><span>until</span><span>(</span><span>d2</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>.</span><span>get_days</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>29</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Flipping d1 and d2 gives the same result,
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// but negative.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span> </span><span>d2</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>get_days</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span>29</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Durations can also be added to dates:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// This convenient syntax comes from the
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// `ToSpan` extension trait.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>29.</span><span>days</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d1</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Generally speaking, whenever there is an operation involving a <code>Span</code>, there is
an equivalent operation available with <code>SignedDuration</code>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>SignedDuration</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>dt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>sdur</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>SignedDuration</span>::<span>from_mins</span><span>(</span><span>90</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>dt1</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>sdur</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>19</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Notice that in the above example, we actually switched from using a
<code>civil::Date</code> to a <code>civil::DateTime</code>. This was done to show how physical time
units can be added. But note that the same can be accomplished with a <code>Span</code>,
because it combines all units:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>dt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>1.</span><span>hour</span><span>().</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>dt1</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>19</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Calendar and physical time units can
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// be combined!
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>1.</span><span>month</span><span>().</span><span>hours</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>dt1</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>19</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>In brief, use a <code>Span</code> when you can. But when it’s too bulky or too slow, use
a <code>SignedDuration</code>. One of the key benefits of a <code>Span</code> is that since it
represents each unit value distinctly, one can preserve the original meaning
of a duration. For example, it’s common to represent the runtime of films in
minutes, even when the duration exceeds one hour. Indeed, a span of 2 hours
is a distinct span from a span of 120 minutes:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>ToSpan</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2.</span><span>hours</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>120.</span><span>minutes</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// This means the spans are distinct values
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// in memory.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_ne!</span><span>(</span><span>span1</span><span>.</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>span2</span><span>.</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>());</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Span.html#method.fieldwise"><code>Span::fieldwise</code></a> routine converts a <code>Span</code> into a value whose units can
be compared in a field-by-field fashion. To compare spans based on the actual
physical duration they represent, use <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Span.html#method.compare"><code>Span::compare</code></a>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2.</span><span>hours</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>120.</span><span>minutes</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert!</span><span>(</span><span>span1</span><span>.</span><span>compare</span><span>(</span><span>span2</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>.</span><span>is_eq</span><span>());</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>This was a very brief introduction to durations in Jiff. They are <em>a lot</em> more
powerful than what is shown here. You might even try playing with the examples
above to explore its nooks and crannies. We’ll cover their API in more detail
in a later section.</p>
<h3 id="when-should-i-use-jiff">When should I use Jiff?</h3>
<p>Jiff isn’t always the right thing to use. Sometimes you can get by with
something simpler.</p>
<p>First and foremost, if you’re <em>measuring</em> the time something takes in a
program, you should almost certainly be using monotonic time. This is
available via <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/time/struct.Instant.html"><code>std::time::Instant</code></a>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>std</span>::<span>time</span>::<span>{</span><span>Duration</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Instant</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>start</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Instant</span>::<span>now</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// do some serious work
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>std</span>::<span>thread</span>::<span>sleep</span><span>(</span><span>Duration</span>::<span>from_secs</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>elapsed</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Instant</span>::<span>now</span><span>().</span><span>duration_since</span><span>(</span><span>start</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>println!</span><span>(</span><span>"</span><span>{elapsed:?}</span><span>"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>If you do need to compute elapsed time using your system clock, then Jiff
is very convenient for that:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>std</span>::<span>time</span>::<span>Duration</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>Timestamp</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// assume `start` came from somewhere
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// over a network
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>start</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Timestamp</span>::<span>now</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>std</span>::<span>thread</span>::<span>sleep</span><span>(</span><span>Duration</span>::<span>from_secs</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>elapsed</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Timestamp</span>::<span>now</span><span>().</span><span>duration_since</span><span>(</span><span>start</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>println!</span><span>(</span><span>"</span><span>{elapsed}</span><span>"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>But you don’t actually need Jiff for this. A <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/time/struct.SystemTime.html"><code>std::time::SystemTime</code></a> will
work perfectly fine:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>std</span>::<span>time</span>::<span>{</span><span>Duration</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>SystemTime</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// assume `start` came from somewhere
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// over a network
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>start</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>SystemTime</span>::<span>now</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>std</span>::<span>thread</span>::<span>sleep</span><span>(</span><span>Duration</span>::<span>from_secs</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>elapsed</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>SystemTime</span>::<span>now</span><span>().</span><span>duration_since</span><span>(</span><span>start</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>println!</span><span>(</span><span>"</span><span>{elapsed:?}</span><span>"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Where Jiff starts to become useful is when you need something beyond just an
integer number of seconds since the Unix epoch. For example, some very hairy
math is required in order to convert a <code>SystemTime</code> into an RFC 3339 timestamp
like <code>2026-11-14T21:34:00Z</code>.</p>
<p>As long as you can live in UTC completely, don’t care about local time and can
work with Unix timestamps only, then the Rust standard library won’t steer you
wrong.</p>
<h2 id="parsing-and-formatting">Parsing and Formatting</h2>
<p>Conversion to and from strings is a major part of Jiff. Jiff’s focus is
primarily on interoperability via the following:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc3339/">RFC 3339</a> specifies a format for fixed offset instants in time, in terms of
a human readable civil datetime. For example, <code>2026-11-12T15:36:00-05:00</code>. This
is the serialization format used by <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Timestamp.html"><code>Timestamp</code></a>.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9557/">RFC 9557</a> extends and updates RFC 3339 to add time zone information.
For example, <code>2026-11-12T15:36:00-05:00[America/New_York]</code>. This is the
serialization format used by <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTime.html"><code>ZonedDateTime</code></a>.</li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601">ISO 8601</a> covers mostly everything else, including durations and
civil datetimes. For example, <code>P5Y3MT20H</code> is an ISO 8601 duration and
<code>2026-11-12T15:30:00</code> is an ISO 8601 civil datetime whose corresponding instant
in time is unknown.</li>
</ul>
<p>What Jiff actually implements is the
<a href="https://tc39.es/proposal-temporal/#sec-temporal-iso8601grammar">Temporal RFC 9557 / ISO 8601</a> grammar for flexible parsing of datetimes from
strings. For most use cases, it suffices to think in terms of RFC 3339,
RFC 9557 or ISO 8601. But Jiff does support parsing strings that aren’t valid
in any of those three specifications. For example, <code>2026-11-12[America/New_York]</code>
parses as if it was <code>2026-11-12T00:00:00[America/New_York]</code>.</p>
<p>In this section, we’ll take a tour of how Jiff does datetime formatting and
printing.</p>
<h3 id="fromstr-and-display"><code>FromStr</code> and <code>Display</code></h3>
<p>Every datetime and duration type in Jiff implements the standard library
<a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/str/trait.FromStr.html"><code>FromStr</code></a> and <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/fmt/trait.Display.html"><code>Display</code></a> traits. This means that it’s natural to parse
datetimes:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>ts</span>: <span>Timestamp</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"2026-10-31T05:15:00Z"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>and print them:</p>
<p>Parsing is flexible, so even if there are components missing, parsing still
succeeds. For example, without minutes or seconds, those components default to
zero:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>dt</span>: <span>civil</span>::<span>DateTime</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"2026-10-31T05"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-10-31T05:00:00"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Another example of flexible parsing is that if a string <em>contains</em> another date
type, then one can parse that. For example, one can parse a date out of an
ISO 8601 datetime:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d</span>: <span>civil</span>::<span>Date</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"2026-10-31T05:30"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-10-31"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>A <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTime.html"><code>ZonedDateTime</code></a> always uses <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9557/">RFC 9557</a> syntax, which includes the IANA time
zone identifier:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span>: <span>ZonedDateTime</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"2026-10-31T05:30[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// While the string we parsed didn't have any offset,
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// one was automatically added by consulting the time
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// zone definition for the civil datetime provided.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-10-31T05:30:00+11:00[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>If you want to print a zoned datetime using only <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc3339/">RFC 3339</a> syntax, then you
can do so by formatting the timestamp directly with the zoned datetime’s
offset:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span>: <span>ZonedDateTime</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"2026-10-31T05:30[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>timestamp</span><span>().</span><span>display_with_offset</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>offset</span><span>()).</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>"2026-10-31T05:30:00+11:00"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Span.html"><code>Span</code></a> and <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.SignedDuration.html"><code>SignedDuration</code></a> types also support the <code>FromStr</code> and
<code>Display</code> traits via the ISO 8601 duration format. For example:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>SignedDuration</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Span</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>duration</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"PT5H30M"</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span>: <span>Span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>duration</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT5H30M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>sdur</span>: <span>SignedDuration</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>duration</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>sdur</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT5H30M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Since a <code>Span</code> is a hybrid duration that also supports calendar units, it can
parse durations with calendar units and physical time units. A <code>SignedDuration</code>
can only parse durations with physical time units:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>SignedDuration</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Span</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>duration</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"P11Y2M28DT5H30M"</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span>: <span>Span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>duration</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"P11Y2M28DT5H30M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>err</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>duration</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span>::<span>&lt;</span><span>SignedDuration</span><span>&gt;</span><span>().</span><span>unwrap_err</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>err</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"parsing ISO 8601 duration in this context requires </span><span>\</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>         that the duration contain a time component </span><span>\</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>         and no components of days or greater"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>The ISO 8601 duration format does, however, have some shortcomings that we’ll
discuss in the next section.</p>
<h3 id="the-friendly-duration-format">The “friendly” Duration Format</h3>
<p>Because ISO 8601 duration strings enjoy broad support, it is the default in
Jiff for both <code>Span</code> and <code>SignedDuration</code>. However, it has two important
shortcomings:</p>
<ol>
<li>It is difficult to read. For example, in <code>P11Y2M28DT5H30M</code>, the lack of
whitespace makes the unit values and the unit labels all run together.</li>
<li>An ISO 8601 duration cannot unambiguously represent all possible <code>Span</code>
values.</li>
</ol>
<p>The second shortcoming is best seen with an example. Consider a <code>Span</code> that is
2000 milliseconds. If you convert this to an ISO 8601 duration string and then
parse it back, you end up with 2 seconds instead:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>Span</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>original</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2000.</span><span>milliseconds</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>original</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT2S"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>got</span>: <span>Span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"PT2S"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// The spans have different unit values!
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>original</span><span>.</span><span>get_seconds</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>original</span><span>.</span><span>get_milliseconds</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>2000</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>got</span><span>.</span><span>get_seconds</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>got</span><span>.</span><span>get_milliseconds</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>This might not be desirable for cases where you want a span’s string format
to faithfully represent all possible distinct values of a span.</p>
<p>For these two reasons, Jiff comes with its own bespoke “friendly” duration
format. The friendly format is non-lossy <em>and</em> is meant to be easier to read
for English speakers than the ISO 8601 duration format. Here are some examples
that are all valid friendly durations:</p>
<pre tabindex="0"><code>40d
40 days
1y1d
1yr 1d
3d4h59m
3 days, 4 hours, 59 minutes
3d 4h 59m
2h30m
2h 30m
1mo
1w
1 week
1w4d
1 wk 4 days
1m
0.0021s
0s
0d
0 days
3 mins 34s 123ms
3 mins 34.123 secs
3 mins 34,123s
1y1mo1d1h1m1.1s
1yr 1mo 1day 1hr 1min 1.1sec
1 year, 1 month, 1 day, 1 hour, 1 minute 1.1 seconds
1 year, 1 month, 1 day, 01:01:01.1
</code></pre><p>Parsing a friendly duration is as easy as parsing an ISO 8601 duration: the
<code>FromStr</code> implementations on both <code>Span</code> and <code>SignedDuration</code> support ISO 8601
durations and friendly durations simultaneously:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span>: <span>Span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"6y 10mo 3d 23h"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// Recall that `Span::fieldwise` permits
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// equality comparisons on `Span` in a
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// unit-by-unit fashion.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>6.</span><span>years</span><span>().</span><span>months</span><span>(</span><span>10</span><span>).</span><span>days</span><span>(</span><span>3</span><span>).</span><span>hours</span><span>(</span><span>23</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Printing a friendly duration does require a bit more effort, since it isn’t
the default. Instead of <code>Span::to_string()</code>, one can use the “alternative”
syntax via Rust’s formatting machinery:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2000.</span><span>milliseconds</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// This also demonstrates that our unit
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// values are preserved.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>format!</span><span>(</span><span>"</span><span>{span:#}</span><span>"</span><span>),</span><span> </span><span>"2000ms"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>One can also use a lower level friendly printer to customize its output.
For example, here’s how to write a span in the friendly format to an existing
<code>String</code> using more spacing, longer unit labels and commas separating each
unit:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>fmt</span>::<span>friendly</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>static</span><span> </span><span>PRINTER</span>: <span>friendly</span>::<span>SpanPrinter</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>friendly</span>::<span>SpanPrinter</span>::<span>new</span><span>()</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>comma_after_designator</span><span>(</span><span>true</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>spacing</span><span>(</span><span>friendly</span>::<span>Spacing</span>::<span>BetweenUnitsAndDesignators</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>designator</span><span>(</span><span>friendly</span>::<span>Designator</span>::<span>Verbose</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>6.</span><span>years</span><span>().</span><span>months</span><span>(</span><span>10</span><span>).</span><span>days</span><span>(</span><span>3</span><span>).</span><span>hours</span><span>(</span><span>23</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>mut</span><span> </span><span>buf</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>String</span>::<span>new</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>PRINTER</span><span>.</span><span>print_span</span><span>(</span><span>&amp;</span><span>span</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>&amp;</span><span>mut</span><span> </span><span>buf</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>buf</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>"6 years, 10 months, 3 days, 23 hours"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>See the <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/fmt/friendly/index.html"><code>jiff::fmt::friendly</code></a> module for a lot more detail, including a
grammar that precisely specifies the format supported.</p>
<h3 id="strptime-and-strftime"><code>strptime</code> and <code>strftime</code></h3>
<p>The <a href="https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/009695399/functions/strptime.html">POSIX <code>strptime</code></a> and <a href="https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/009695399/functions/strftime.html">POSIX <code>strftime</code></a> routines are far from the ideal
way to format and parse datetimes, but they are ubiquitous and most programmers
are familiar with them. Because of their ubiquity, and because of the utility
of being able to format and parse bespoke datetime formats, Jiff provides
implementations of these routines that are mostly POSIX compatible.</p>
<p>Every datetime type in Jiff comes with <code>strptime</code> and <code>strftime</code> routines.
For example, to parse a civil date in the US <code>month/day/year</code> format:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>Date</span>::<span>strptime</span><span>(</span><span>"%m/%d/%Y"</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>"11/13/2026"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>date</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-11-13"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Or to format a zoned datetime in a convenient to read representation for
English speakers:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>13</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"Australia/Sydney"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>strftime</span><span>(</span><span>"%a, %b %d, %Y %H:%M %Z"</span><span>).</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>"Fri, Nov 13, 2026 17:30 AEDT"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Callers can also use lower level APIs from <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/fmt/strtime/index.html"><code>jiff::fmt::strtime</code></a> to get access
to each parsed component individually:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>fmt</span>::<span>strtime</span>::<span>BrokenDownTime</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>tm</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>BrokenDownTime</span>::<span>parse</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"%a, %b %d, %Y %H:%M"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"Fri, Nov 13, 2026 17:30"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>tm</span><span>.</span><span>weekday</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>Some</span><span>(</span><span>civil</span>::<span>Weekday</span>::<span>Friday</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>tm</span><span>.</span><span>month</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>Some</span><span>(</span><span>11</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>tm</span><span>.</span><span>day</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>Some</span><span>(</span><span>13</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>tm</span><span>.</span><span>year</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>Some</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>tm</span><span>.</span><span>hour</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>Some</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>tm</span><span>.</span><span>minute</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>Some</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>tm</span><span>.</span><span>second</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>None</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="the-jifffmt-module">The <code>jiff::fmt</code> module</h3>
<p>While the above tour should cover most use cases, there are sometimes reasons
for exerting more control over parsing or formatting. Or for supporting
alternate formats like RFC 2822. The <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/fmt/index.html"><code>jiff::fmt</code></a> module is meant to cover
these use cases at the cost of more complex APIs. For example, consider parsing
an <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc2822/">RFC 2822</a> datetime string from a <code>&amp;[u8]</code> (which lets callers avoid a UTF-8
validation check):</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>fmt</span>::<span>rfc2822</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>static</span><span> </span><span>PARSER</span>: <span>rfc2822</span>::<span>DateTimeParser</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>rfc2822</span>::<span>DateTimeParser</span>::<span>new</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>data</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>b</span><span>"Sat, 13 Jul 2024 15:09:59 -0400"</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>ts</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>PARSER</span><span>.</span><span>parse_timestamp</span><span>(</span><span>data</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>ts</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2024-07-13T19:09:59Z"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Another example shows how to change the default disambiguation strategy when
the provided civil datetime is ambiguous. For example, Sydney, Australia moved
the clocks backwards at <code>2026-04-05T03:00:00</code> local time. That is, they went
from UTC offset <code>+11</code> to UTC offset <code>+10</code>. So what happens if we try to parse
a time on the clock that is repeated as a result?</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>ZonedDateTime</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span>: <span>ZonedDateTime</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30:00+11:00[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Here, the first occurrence of <code>02:30</code> is selected, just before Sydney leaves
daylight saving time. Now, the data itself can actually select the desired
offset:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>ZonedDateTime</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span>: <span>ZonedDateTime</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30+11:00[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30:00+11:00[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span>: <span>ZonedDateTime</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30+10:00[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30:00+10:00[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>But this happens because <em>there is no ambiguity</em> in the data. Since the offset
is supplied, the exact instant in time is known. Going back to
<code>2026-04-05T02:30[Australia/Sydney]</code>, which <em>is</em> ambiguous because it could
refer to two different instants in time, how do we modify the strategy for
resolving that ambiguity? We can’t using the standard library <code>FromStr</code> trait,
so we instead have to construct our own parser manually, just like we did for
<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc2822/">RFC 2822</a> parsing above. This example shows how to do that and all of the
different disambiguation options. Remember, UTC offset +11:00 corresponds to
the first occurrence of <code>2026-04-05T02:30</code> on the clocks of folks in Sydney
while UTC offset +10:00 corresponds to the second occurrence.</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>fmt</span>::<span>temporal</span>::<span>DateTimeParser</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>tz</span>::<span>Disambiguation</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>string</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// "compatible" is the default strategy, which selects
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// the earlier time in a fold (usually moving from daylight
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// saving time to standard time) and the later time in a
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// gap (usually moving from standard time to daylight
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// saving time).
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>parser</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>DateTimeParser</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>disambiguation</span><span>(</span><span>Disambiguation</span>::<span>Compatible</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>parser</span><span>.</span><span>parse_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>string</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30:00+11:00[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// "earlier" always selects the first occurrence
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// in a fold, or the time before a gap.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// So this is the same as compatible in this case,
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// since our civil time is in a fold.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>parser</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>DateTimeParser</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>disambiguation</span><span>(</span><span>Disambiguation</span>::<span>Earlier</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>parser</span><span>.</span><span>parse_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>string</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30:00+11:00[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// "later" always selects the second occurrence
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// in a fold, or the time after a gap.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// So this is different from "compatible" for a
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// fold. Notice that the UTC +10:00 offset is
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// chosen.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>parser</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>DateTimeParser</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>disambiguation</span><span>(</span><span>Disambiguation</span>::<span>Later</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>parser</span><span>.</span><span>parse_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>string</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2026-04-05T02:30:00+10:00[Australia/Sydney]"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// "reject" means that if the instant is ambiguous,
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// then an error is returned.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>parser</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>DateTimeParser</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>disambiguation</span><span>(</span><span>Disambiguation</span>::<span>Reject</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>err</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>parser</span><span>.</span><span>parse_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>string</span><span>).</span><span>unwrap_err</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>err</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"error converting datetime to instant </span><span>\</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>         in time zone Australia/Sydney: </span><span>\</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>         datetime is ambiguous since it falls </span><span>\</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>         into a fold between offsets +11 and +10"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/fmt/index.html"><code>jiff::fmt</code></a> module contains lots of other goodies that we won’t cover
here, including, but not limited to:</p>
<ul>
<li>The ability to format directly into an existing allocation.</li>
<li>Parsing a datetime into its individual components.</li>
<li>Helpers for <a href="https://serde.rs/"><code>serde</code></a> integration.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="locale-sensitive-formatting-via-icu4x-integration">Locale Sensitive Formatting via ICU4X Integration</h3>
<p>No section on datetime formatting would be complete without addressing locales.
In this context, a locale is a set of user preferences used for formatting
datetimes. This is useful when you want to present a datetime to an end user
that doesn’t expect standard formats from <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc3339/">RFC 3339</a>, <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9557/">RFC 9557</a> or <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601">ISO 8601</a>.</p>
<p>Jiff itself does not provide any facilities for dealing with locales. Instead,
users can opt into this support via the <a href="https://docs.rs/icu">ICU4X</a> project. To make conversions
between Jiff and ICU4X, the <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff-icu"><code>jiff-icu</code></a> integration crate can be used.</p>
<p>Here’s an example showing how to format a zoned datetime in the <code>fr-LA</code> locale:
(You’ll need to run <code>cargo add icu jiff-icu</code> to your project for this to work.)</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>icu</span>::<span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>calendar</span>::<span>Iso</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>datetime</span>::<span>{</span><span>fieldsets</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>DateTimeFormatter</span><span>},</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>locale</span>::<span>locale</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>time</span>::<span>{</span><span>zone</span>::<span>models</span>::<span>AtTime</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>TimeZoneInfo</span><span>},</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>ZonedDateTime</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff_icu</span>::<span>ConvertFrom</span><span> </span><span>as</span><span> </span><span>_</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Firstly, do a conversion from Jiff's zoned datetime to ICU's.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>jiff_zdt</span>: <span>ZonedDateTime</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"2024-09-10T23:37:20-04[America/New_York]"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>icu_zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>icu</span>::<span>time</span>::<span>ZonedDateTime</span>::<span>&lt;</span><span>Iso</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>TimeZoneInfo</span><span>&lt;</span><span>AtTime</span><span>&gt;&gt;</span>::<span>convert_from</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>&amp;</span><span>jiff_zdt</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Second, create a datetime formatter for the fr-LA locale:
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>formatter</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>DateTimeFormatter</span>::<span>try_new</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>locale!</span><span>(</span><span>"fr-LA"</span><span>).</span><span>into</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>fieldsets</span>::<span>YMDET</span>::<span>medium</span><span>().</span><span>with_zone</span><span>(</span><span>fieldsets</span>::<span>zone</span>::<span>SpecificLong</span><span>),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Thirdly, format the zoned datetime.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>formatter</span><span>.</span><span>format</span><span>(</span><span>&amp;</span><span>icu_zdt</span><span>).</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"mar. 10 sept. 2024, 23:37:20 heure d’été de l’Est nord-américain"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>The ICU4X API has a lot of options for customizing datetime formatting.
Exploring it further is outside the scope of this blog.</p>
<h2 id="date-manipulation">Date Manipulation</h2>
<p>Jiff provides many methods on its datetime types to make using them as
comfortable and error-free as possible.</p>
<h3 id="date-basics">Date Basics</h3>
<p>Every datetime type provides a <code>new</code> constructor that lets callers build it
from its constituent parts. In most cases, this constructor is fallible. For
example:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>civil</span>::<span>Date</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Date</span>::<span>new</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>29</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// All datetime types have convenient
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// accessors for each of their components.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>year</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>2024</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>month</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>day</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>29</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>err</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Date</span>::<span>new</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>29</span><span>).</span><span>unwrap_err</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>err</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"parameter 'day' for `2025-02` is invalid, must be in range `1..=28`"</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Because creating dates is so common, there is a shorthand for doing so when
you know the inputs are valid:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>const</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>29</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>year</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>2024</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>month</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>day</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>29</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>That is, <code>civil::date</code> is a const function that will panic if its inputs are
invalid. It should only be used with inputs that are known to be valid.
It would, for example, be inappropriate to use this with user provided values.
Because of the <code>const</code> block above, if you try to provide invalid values,
you’ll get a compiler error.</p>
<p>For datetime types that represent an instant in time (i.e., <code>ZonedDateTime</code> and
<code>Timestamp</code>), there is also a <code>now()</code> constructor for getting the current time:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>Timestamp</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ZonedDateTime</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>now</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Timestamp</span>::<span>now</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>println!</span><span>(</span><span>"</span><span>{now}</span><span>"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Example output: 2026-11-15T14:17:21.80099287Z
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// This will automatically fetch your system's configured time zone.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>now</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>ZonedDateTime</span>::<span>now</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>println!</span><span>(</span><span>"</span><span>{now}</span><span>"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Example output: 2026-11-15T09:17:21.80102125-05:00[America/New_York]
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>In addition to constructors, new date values can be constructed from old
date values using the <code>Date::with</code> API:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>const</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>29</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// This API doesn't break just because 31
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// is supplied before a month that has 31
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// days. Any order is fine! This is because
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// errors are deferred until `build()` is
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// called.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span>.</span><span>with</span><span>().</span><span>day</span><span>(</span><span>31</span><span>).</span><span>month</span><span>(</span><span>3</span><span>).</span><span>build</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d2</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2024-03-31"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Once you have a date, there are oodles of APIs available:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>10</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>weekday</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>Weekday</span>::<span>Thursday</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>first_of_year</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>last_of_year</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>12</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>first_of_month</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>last_of_month</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>yesterday</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>9</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>tomorrow</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Moreover, because of the <a href="#the-datetime-onion">datetime onion</a>, all of these
APIs are also available on <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.DateTime.html"><code>civil::DateTime</code></a> and <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTime.html"><code>ZonedDateTime</code></a>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>create_zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>|</span><span>date</span>: <span>civil</span>::<span>Date</span><span>|</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>ZonedDateTime</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>Error</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>date</span><span>.</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>create_zdt</span><span>(</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>10</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>weekday</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>Weekday</span>::<span>Thursday</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>first_of_year</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>create_zdt</span><span>(</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>last_of_year</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>create_zdt</span><span>(</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>12</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>first_of_month</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>create_zdt</span><span>(</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>last_of_month</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>create_zdt</span><span>(</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>yesterday</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>create_zdt</span><span>(</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>9</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>tomorrow</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>create_zdt</span><span>(</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>These routines on <code>ZonedDateTime</code> also take time zone transitions into account.
For example, <code>ZonedDateTime</code> knows that <code>tomorrow()</code> doesn’t always add 24
hours:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>// On 2024-11-03 at 2AM in New York, time was rolled
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// back 1 hour in the transition from daylight saving
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// time to standard time. That means 2024-11-03 was
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// actually 25 hours long in New York.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>25</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>tomorrow</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>tomorrow</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>tomorrow</span><span>.</span><span>datetime</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>25</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// The physical time difference between them is 25 hours!
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>tomorrow</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>format!</span><span>(</span><span>"</span><span>{span:#}</span><span>"</span><span>),</span><span> </span><span>"25h"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><blockquote>
<p><strong>NOTE</strong>: We’ll talk about date arithmetic in the next section.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>When <code>ZonedDateTime::tomorrow()</code> (among other APIs that produce a
<code>ZonedDateTime</code>) would land in a gap or a fold, the “compatible” disambiguation
strategy is chosen automatically:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>// For a zoned datetime that lands
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// in a fold, the earlier instant is
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// chosen by default.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>tomorrow</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>tomorrow</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>tomorrow</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>"2024-11-03T01:30:00-04:00[America/New_York]"</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// And for a gap, the later instant
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// is chosen by default.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>9</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>tomorrow</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>tomorrow</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>tomorrow</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>"2024-03-10T03:30:00-04:00[America/New_York]"</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>To override this behavior, you can drop down to civil datetimes, perform the
operation, and then use <code>TimeZone::to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</code> to control the
exact disambiguation strategy:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>// For a zoned datetime that lands
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// in a fold, the earlier instant is
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// chosen by default. But let's say
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// we want the later instant.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>tomorrow</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>.</span><span>time_zone</span><span>()</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>.</span><span>to_ambiguous_zoned_datetime</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>datetime</span><span>().</span><span>tomorrow</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>.</span><span>later</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>tomorrow</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Notice that the offset is now -05,
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// which corresponds to the later
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// instant.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>"2024-11-03T01:30:00-05:00[America/New_York]"</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>There are a few other methods of note on date types that are worth mentioning.
First is the ability to query for things like, “The Thursday after next” or
“The last Sunday of the month.” The former can be answered by the <code>nth_weekday</code>
API:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>10</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt1</span><span>.</span><span>weekday</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>Weekday</span>::<span>Wednesday</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// Not this Thursday, but the next!
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span>.</span><span>nth_weekday</span><span>(</span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>Weekday</span>::<span>Thursday</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt2</span><span>.</span><span>weekday</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>Weekday</span>::<span>Thursday</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt2</span><span>.</span><span>date</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>18</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>While the latter can be answered by the <code>nth_weekday_of_month</code> API:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>10</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt1</span><span>.</span><span>weekday</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>Weekday</span>::<span>Wednesday</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// The last Sunday of this month.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span>.</span><span>nth_weekday_of_month</span><span>(</span><span>-</span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>Weekday</span>::<span>Sunday</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt2</span><span>.</span><span>weekday</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>Weekday</span>::<span>Sunday</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt2</span><span>.</span><span>date</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>28</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Finally, users can access a convenient time series iterator via <code>series</code>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>start</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>10</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>mut</span><span> </span><span>dates</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>vec!</span><span>[];</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Every week for 8 weeks.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>for</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>in</span><span> </span><span>start</span><span>.</span><span>series</span><span>(</span><span>1.</span><span>week</span><span>()).</span><span>take</span><span>(</span><span>8</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>dates</span><span>.</span><span>push</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>dates</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>vec!</span><span>[</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>10</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>17</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>24</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>8</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>8</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>14</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>8</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>21</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>8</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>28</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>]</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>All of these methods are also available on <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.Date.html"><code>civil::Date</code></a> and
<a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.DateTime.html"><code>civil::DateTime</code></a>, which should make it very natural to move between them.</p>
<h3 id="date-arithmetic">Date Arithmetic</h3>
<p>All datetime types in Jiff support adding and subtracting durations, and
finding the span of time between two datetime values. Let’s start with a
<a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.Date.html"><code>civil::Date</code></a>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span> </span><span>+</span><span> </span><span>2.</span><span>days</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>8</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Here we make use of the <code>std::ops::Add</code> trait implementation for <code>civil::Date</code>.
The downside of using this, though, is that if the date value goes out of
range, then it panics. That may or may not be acceptable. But if you’re dealing
with user inputs, then you should use <code>Date::checked_add</code> instead:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>2.</span><span>days</span><span>())</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>8</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Let’s take a look at a few other examples involving months and years:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>1.</span><span>month</span><span>())</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>6</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>2.</span><span>months</span><span>())</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>1.</span><span>month</span><span>())</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>28</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>29</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>1.</span><span>year</span><span>().</span><span>months</span><span>(</span><span>2</span><span>).</span><span>days</span><span>(</span><span>5</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2025</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>4</span><span>));</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>As with other methods on <code>Date</code>, they are also available on <code>ZonedDateTime</code>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>13</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>45</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>1.</span><span>month</span><span>())</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>6</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>13</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>45</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>If we take a look at the type signature for <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTime.html#method.checked_add"><code>ZonedDateTime::checked_add</code></a>, we
can see that it’s actually polymorphic:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>pub</span><span> </span><span>fn</span> <span>checked_add</span><span>&lt;</span><span>A</span>: <span>Into</span><span>&lt;</span><span>ZonedDateTimeArithmetic</span><span>&gt;&gt;</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>&amp;</span><span>self</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>duration</span>: <span>A</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>)</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>ZonedDateTime</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Error</span><span>&gt;</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Specifically, it accepts anything that can be converted into a
<a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTimeArithmetic.html"><code>ZonedDateTimeArithmetic</code></a>. In this case, there are <code>From</code> trait
implementations for the following types:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Span.html"><code>Span</code></a></li>
<li><a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.SignedDuration.html"><code>SignedDuration</code></a></li>
<li><a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/time/struct.Duration.html"><code>std::time::Duration</code></a></li>
</ul>
<p>This means that <code>ZonedDateTime::checked_add</code> can accept any one of these
duration values:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>SignedDuration</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>13</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>45</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>d</span><span>.</span><span>checked_add</span><span>(</span><span>SignedDuration</span>::<span>from_mins</span><span>(</span><span>45</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>14</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>This type of polymorphism is common in Jiff. Here, it is merely used to
provide a convenient way to use multiple duration types. But as we’ll see,
it’s also used to provide an extension point for configuration options that
control how the operation works.</p>
<p>In addition to adding or subtracting durations from datetime values, one can
also compute the duration between two datetime values. For example, to get the
number of days between two dates:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>9</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d2</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>122.</span><span>days</span><span>().</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>());</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Similarly, we can use <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.Date.html#method.until"><code>Date::until</code></a> to compute the span between dates:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>d2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>9</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>d1</span><span>.</span><span>until</span><span>(</span><span>d2</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>122.</span><span>days</span><span>().</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>());</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Like with <code>Date::checked_add</code>, this routine is also available at
<a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTime.html#method.until"><code>ZonedDateTime::until</code></a>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>9</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt1</span><span>.</span><span>until</span><span>(</span><span>&amp;</span><span>zdt2</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2928.</span><span>hours</span><span>().</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>());</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Notice though that hours, instead of days, are returned. This is because two
<code>ZonedDateTime</code> values might be in different time zones. Since a <code>ZonedDateTime</code>
is fundamentally an instant in time, and because days might be of varying length
depending on the time zone, defaulting to hours as the largest possible unit
means subtracting two zoned datetimes will always succeed. However, we can
request larger units in the duration returned by passing additional options.
Notably, <code>ZonedDateTime::until</code> is polymorphic:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>pub</span><span> </span><span>fn</span> <span>until</span><span>&lt;</span><span>'a</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>A</span>: <span>Into</span><span>&lt;</span><span>ZonedDateTimeDifference</span><span>&lt;</span><span>'a</span><span>&gt;&gt;&gt;</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>&amp;</span><span>self</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>other</span>: <span>A</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>)</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>Span</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Error</span><span>&gt;</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>That is, callers aren’t just limited to passing a <code>ZonedDateTime</code> here, but
<em>anything</em> that can be converted to a <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTimeDifference.html"><code>ZonedDateTimeDifference</code></a>. Here is
one such example, where we can override the largest units returned in the
duration:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>9</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>zdt1</span><span>.</span><span>until</span><span>((</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Month</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>&amp;</span><span>zdt2</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>3.</span><span>months</span><span>().</span><span>days</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>).</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>()</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>A <code>ZonedDateTimeDifference</code> provides much more powerful configuration options
for rounding the span returned. Take this example, which produces a span with
many unit values:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>15</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>14</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>54</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>19</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>13</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>27</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>123_456_789</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>zdt1</span><span>.</span><span>until</span><span>((</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Day</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>&amp;</span><span>zdt2</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>16.</span><span>days</span><span>()</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>hours</span><span>(</span><span>4</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>42</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>seconds</span><span>(</span><span>33</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>milliseconds</span><span>(</span><span>123</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>microseconds</span><span>(</span><span>456</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>nanoseconds</span><span>(</span><span>789</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>This might be <em>too</em> much precision to show to an end user. Instead, perhaps
we’d like to <em>round</em> the span to the nearest half-hour. We can do this by
building a <code>ZonedDateTimeDifference</code> explicitly and configuring its options:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>RoundMode</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ZonedDateTimeDifference</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>15</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>14</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>54</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>5</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>31</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>19</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>13</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>27</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>123_456_789</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>difference</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>ZonedDateTimeDifference</span>::<span>new</span><span>(</span><span>&amp;</span><span>zdt2</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>largest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Day</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>smallest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Minute</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>increment</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>mode</span><span>(</span><span>RoundMode</span>::<span>HalfExpand</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>zdt1</span><span>.</span><span>until</span><span>(</span><span>difference</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>16.</span><span>days</span><span>().</span><span>hours</span><span>(</span><span>4</span><span>).</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>).</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Note also that rounding a span takes time zone transitions like daylight saving
time into account. For example, let’s take <code>2024-11-02 17:30</code> and
<code>2024-11-03 04:45</code> in New York. Because New York stopped daylight saving time
at 2AM on 2024-11-03, the amount of physical time between them is 12 hours and
15 minutes:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>4</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>45</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt2</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>12.</span><span>hours</span><span>().</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>15</span><span>).</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>());</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Normally, if we rounded to the nearest day, this would round up to 1 day. But
since the midpoint of this particular day is actually 12 hours and 30 minutes,
this duration will round down to 0 days when we request it:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>RoundMode</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ZonedDateTimeDifference</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>4</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>45</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>difference</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>ZonedDateTimeDifference</span>::<span>new</span><span>(</span><span>&amp;</span><span>zdt2</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>largest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Day</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>smallest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Day</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>mode</span><span>(</span><span>RoundMode</span>::<span>HalfExpand</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt1</span><span>.</span><span>until</span><span>(</span><span>difference</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0.</span><span>days</span><span>().</span><span>fieldwise</span><span>());</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Next we’ll continue our discussion of rounding, but when applied to datetimes
themselves.</p>
<h3 id="date-rounding">Date Rounding</h3>
<p>Date rounding in Jiff is limited to units of days or lower. Therefore, it’s
only available on <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTime.html"><code>ZonedDateTime</code></a>, <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Timestamp.html"><code>Timestamp</code></a>, <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.DateTime.html"><code>civil::DateTime</code></a>,
<a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.Time.html"><code>civil::Time</code></a> but not <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.Date.html"><code>civil::Date</code></a>.</p>
<p>A common example for rounding a datetime is to reduce precision. For example,
a system clock might provide nanosecond precision, but maybe we only care about
the nearest half-hour:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>2</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>46</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>20</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>123_456_789</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>.</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"2024-11-02T17:46:20.123456789-04:00[America/New_York]"</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Round to the nearest half hour.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>rounded</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>((</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Minute</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>rounded</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>"2024-11-02T18:00:00-04:00[America/New_York]"</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>As with rounding spans, rounding a <code>ZonedDateTime</code> is aware of time zone
transitions:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2024</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>.</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>11</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>45</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>20</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>123_456_789</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>.</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"America/New_York"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>// Round to the nearest day.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>rounded</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Day</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>rounded</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>"2024-11-04T00:00:00-05:00[America/New_York]"</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Notice that in the above example, our time of 11:45AM is <em>before</em> noon, yet the
result still rounds <em>up</em> to the next day. This is because the midpoint for this
day is 11:30AM since, by 11:30AM, 12.5 hours have already elapsed because of
the repeated 1AM hour in the move away from daylight saving time in New York.</p>
<h3 id="other-calendars">Other Calendars</h3>
<p>Jiff itself doesn’t support any calendars other than the proleptic Gregorian
calendar. One can instead use the <a href="https://docs.rs/icu">ICU4X</a> crate to work in non-Gregorian
calendars, and convert between datetime types using the <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff-icu"><code>jiff-icu</code></a> crate:
(Use <code>cargo add icu jiff-icu</code> to make this example work.)</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>icu</span>::<span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>calendar</span>::<span>{</span><span>types</span>::<span>Month</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Date</span><span> </span><span>as</span><span> </span><span>IcuDate</span><span>},</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>time</span>::<span>{</span><span>DateTime</span><span> </span><span>as</span><span> </span><span>IcuDateTime</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Time</span><span> </span><span>as</span><span> </span><span>IcuTime</span><span>},</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>civil</span>::<span>DateTime</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff_icu</span>::<span>ConvertTryFrom</span><span> </span><span>as</span><span> </span><span>_</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Firstly, create an ICU datetime using the Hebrew calendar.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>datetime</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>IcuDateTime</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>date</span>: <span>IcuDate</span>::<span>try_new_hebrew_v2</span><span>(</span><span>5785</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Month</span>::<span>new</span><span>(</span><span>5</span><span>),</span><span> </span><span>4</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>time</span>: <span>IcuTime</span>::<span>try_new</span><span>(</span><span>17</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>30</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Secondly, convert it to a civil datetime.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// This will automatically use ICU to convert
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// the date from the Hebrew calendar to the
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>// Gregorian (or ISO) calendar.
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>DateTime</span>::<span>convert_try_from</span><span>(</span><span>datetime</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>zdt</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2025-02-02T17:30:00"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>The ICU4X API supports <a href="https://docs.rs/icu_calendar/latest/icu_calendar/">many more calendars</a>.</p>
<h3 id="which-datetime-type-do-i-use">Which DateTime Type Do I Use?</h3>
<p>There are 3 potentially confusable datetime types in Jiff:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/civil/struct.DateTime.html"><code>civil::DateTime</code></a></li>
<li><a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Timestamp.html"><code>Timestamp</code></a></li>
<li><a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.ZonedDateTime.html"><code>ZonedDateTime</code></a></li>
</ul>
<p>When should you use each one versus the other?</p>
<p>To a first approximation, if you <em>can</em> use a <code>ZonedDateTime</code>, then that is
probably the type you want to reach for. It represents a specific instant in
time in a particular geographic location. That is, it has both a <code>Timestamp</code>
and a <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/tz/struct.TimeZone.html"><code>TimeZone</code></a>.</p>
<p>If you only ever need to work in UTC or just deal with system time, then a
<code>Timestamp</code> is probably the right choice. This is useful for representing a
specific instant in time that is not connected to any time zone.</p>
<p>A <code>civil::DateTime</code> is useful in contexts where you don’t care about a specific
instant in time and when calcuations should be done independent of time zone.
For example, when parsing datetime information from a user interface datetime
picker.</p>
<p>Remember that both a <code>civil::DateTime</code> and a <code>Timestamp</code> can be converted to a
<code>ZonedDateTime</code> by supplying a time zone.</p>
<h2 id="span-manipulation">Span Manipulation</h2>
<p>We’ve already seen how to add spans to datetime values and how to get spans
as a difference between datetime values. But we haven’t yet explored the APIs
available on a <code>Span</code> itself.</p>
<p>First and foremost, we can build a <code>Span</code> through its constructor, which
returns a <code>Span</code> with all of its units set to <code>0</code>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>Span</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Span</span>::<span>new</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>A <code>Span</code> is both <code>Copy</code> and immutable. We can create new spans through method
chaining:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Span</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>hours</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT1H30M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Or we can use the <code>ToSpan</code> convenience trait, which adds methods on integer
types for constructing spans:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>ToSpan</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>1.</span><span>hour</span><span>().</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT1H30M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Note though that methods like <code>Span::hours</code> and <code>ToSpan::hours</code> will panic
when the integer is outside the limits of what <code>Span</code> supports. This is fine
when your spans are literals in your program, since a panic would indicate a
bug. But if your inputs come from an end user, it’s better to use fallible
methods:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>Span</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>try_hours</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>.</span><span>try_minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT1H30M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Spans preserve each individual unit value. This means that 90 minutes and 1
hour 30 minutes, while they represent the same duration, are actually different
values in memory:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>1.</span><span>hour</span><span>().</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>90.</span><span>minutes</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span1</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT1H30M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span2</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT90M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>This permits callers maximum flexibility when it comes to representing
durations. This makes it possible to represent durations in a specific unit
even when they add up to larger units. For example, the runtime duration of
films is typically conveyed in minutes, even when the runtime exceeds one hour.
This can be adjusted programmatically using <code>Span::round</code>:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>SpanRound</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>1.</span><span>hour</span><span>().</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>span1</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>(</span><span>SpanRound</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>largest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Minute</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span2</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT90M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>You can go the other way as well:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>SpanRound</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>90.</span><span>minutes</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>span1</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>(</span><span>SpanRound</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>largest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Hour</span><span>))</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span2</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"PT1H30M"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Neither of these examples do actual rounding, despite the name of the API.
Instead, since we’re only setting the largest unit, this API is doing unit
balancing. Unit balancing is when all unit values rollover into larger units
up until the largest configured unit. This works even for calendar units too,
but you need to provide a relative date:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>SpanRound</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2756.</span><span>hours</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>dt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2020</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>round</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>SpanRound</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>largest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Year</span><span>).</span><span>relative</span><span>(</span><span>dt</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>(</span><span>round</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>.</span><span>to_friendly</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"3mo 23d 20h"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Without a relative date, it’s not possible to know how many hours are in a
single month. That is, once calendar units are involved, a span cannot be
interpreted as a physical duration of time until some context is applied.</p>
<p>The same even applies to days themselves, since days can sometimes be more or
less than 24 hours. Take the above example. What happens if we use a zoned
datetime as our relative date instead?</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>SpanRound</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2756.</span><span>hours</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>zdt</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2020</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>at</span><span>(</span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>).</span><span>in_tz</span><span>(</span><span>"Europe/Rome"</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>round</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>SpanRound</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>largest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Year</span><span>).</span><span>relative</span><span>(</span><span>&amp;</span><span>zdt</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>(</span><span>round</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>.</span><span>to_friendly</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"3mo 23d 21h"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Notice that the duration’s hour component changed from <code>20</code> to <code>21</code>. Why?
Because we started with 2756 hour in both cases and because <code>2020-03-29</code> in
Rome was only 23 hours long (in a move from standard time to daylight saving
time). That means that one of those days in <code>3mo 23d</code> was only 23 hours long.
So an extra hour needed to be added to make up for it.</p>
<p>The <code>round</code> API can also do actual rounding:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>SpanRound</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2.</span><span>hours</span><span>().</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>round</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>SpanRound</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>smallest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Hour</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>(</span><span>round</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>.</span><span>to_friendly</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"3h"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>You can also change its rounding mode from the default (“half-expand”, or like
you were taught rounding in school) to truncation (or one of several other
modes):</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>RoundMode</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>SpanRound</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2.</span><span>hours</span><span>().</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>round</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>SpanRound</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>smallest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Hour</span><span>).</span><span>mode</span><span>(</span><span>RoundMode</span>::<span>Trunc</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>(</span><span>round</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>.</span><span>to_friendly</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2h"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>One can also specify a rounding increment to round to the nearest unit value.
For example, to the nearest half-hour:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>SpanRound</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2.</span><span>hours</span><span>().</span><span>minutes</span><span>(</span><span>31</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>round</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>SpanRound</span>::<span>new</span><span>().</span><span>smallest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Minute</span><span>).</span><span>increment</span><span>(</span><span>30</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>(</span><span>round</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>.</span><span>to_friendly</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"2h 30m"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Because a <code>Span</code> value cannot easily be compared correctly based purely on its
value in memory, a dedicated comparison <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/struct.Span.html#method.compare"><code>Span::compare</code></a> routine is provided:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span1</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>2.</span><span>hours</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span2</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>120.</span><span>minutes</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>assert!</span><span>(</span><span>span1</span><span>.</span><span>compare</span><span>(</span><span>span2</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>.</span><span>is_eq</span><span>());</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Also, one can convert a <code>Span</code> to a floating point value in terms of any unit.
For example:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>90.</span><span>minutes</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>total</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Hour</span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1.5</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>Converting to calendar units requires a relative date for the same reasons
discussed above:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>civil</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>24.</span><span>hours</span><span>();</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>total</span><span>((</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Month</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>6</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>)))</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>0.03333333333333333</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>total</span><span>((</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Month</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>civil</span>::<span>date</span><span>(</span><span>2026</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>7</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>)))</span><span>?</span><span>,</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>0.03225806451612903</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
<p>So far, we’ve explored a good chunk of Jiff’s API. We didn’t talk about every
nook and cranny, but I think we’ve covered the most important pieces.</p>
<p>We’ll wrap up by briefly discussing motivation and inspiration.</p>
<h3 id="motivation">Motivation</h3>
<p>Whenever I needed datetime handling in Rust, I always got confused. Should I
use <code>chrono</code> or <code>time</code>? How do I get my local time from a timestamp? How do I
use my system’s time zone database? How do I do calendar arithmetic?</p>
<p>Before I started working on Jiff, this confusion got annoying enough for me to
consider writing my own crate. I felt like the APIs of both <code>chrono</code> and <code>time</code>
could be improved upon. I personally found the domain quite interesting and so
set out to build something better.</p>
<p>Why not improve on an existing solution, like <code>chrono</code> or <code>time</code>, instead of
adding yet another choice into the ecosystem? I do think there are material
downsides of having too many choices, but I had concluded that both <code>chrono</code>
and <code>time</code> (in 2024) had reached a local maximum. Moreover, in 2024, I was not
an expert in datetime handling. It took building a datetime library to become
one. So convincing crate maintainers to go in a radically different direction
would have been a tall order.</p>
<p>Jiff’s <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/_documentation/design/index.html">design</a> and <a href="https://docs.rs/jiff/1.*/jiff/_documentation/comparison/index.html">comparison</a> documentation contain much more detail
regarding the motivation and specific examples between Jiff, <code>chrono</code> and
<code>time</code>.</p>
<h3 id="inspiration">Inspiration</h3>
<p>Jiff is heavily inspired by <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Temporal">Temporal</a>, which is JavaScript’s standard library
solution for date and time management. Jiff inherits API design, functionality
and even parsing/formatting of datetime values. Note though that Jiff is not
and does not aim to be a 100% compatible implementation of the Temporal
specification. Jiff is meant to be a Rust ecosystem library first and foremost.</p>
<p>With that said, if you’re familiar with Temporal and Rust, then it’s likely
you’ll find Jiff to be a relatively straightforward translation of Temporal’s
API to Rust. This example shows how to use Temporal to round a calendar span
to the nearest number of days relative to a date:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="javascript"><span><span><span>const</span> <span>duration</span> <span>=</span> <span>Temporal</span><span>.</span><span>Duration</span><span>.</span><span>from</span><span>({</span> <span>months</span><span>:</span> <span>1</span><span>,</span> <span>days</span><span>:</span> <span>1</span><span>,</span> <span>hours</span><span>:</span> <span>1</span> <span>});</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>const</span> <span>roundedDuration</span> <span>=</span> <span>duration</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>({</span>
</span></span><span><span>  <span>largestUnit</span><span>:</span> <span>"days"</span><span>,</span>
</span></span><span><span>  <span>smallestUnit</span><span>:</span> <span>"days"</span><span>,</span>
</span></span><span><span>  <span>relativeTo</span><span>:</span> <span>Temporal</span><span>.</span><span>PlainDateTime</span><span>.</span><span>from</span><span>(</span><span>"2022-01-01"</span><span>),</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>});</span>
</span></span><span><span><span>console</span><span>.</span><span>log</span><span>(</span><span>roundedDuration</span><span>);</span> <span>// "P32D"
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>And here’s the equivalent operation in Jiff:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code data-lang="rust"><span><span><span>use</span><span> </span><span>jiff</span>::<span>{</span><span>SpanRound</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>ToSpan</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>Unit</span><span>};</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>fn</span> <span>main</span><span>()</span><span> </span>-&gt; <span>anyhow</span>::<span>Result</span><span>&lt;</span><span>()</span><span>&gt;</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>1.</span><span>month</span><span>().</span><span>days</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>).</span><span>hours</span><span>(</span><span>1</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>let</span><span> </span><span>rounded_span</span><span> </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>span</span><span>.</span><span>round</span><span>(</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>        </span><span>SpanRound</span>::<span>new</span><span>()</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>largest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Day</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>smallest</span><span>(</span><span>Unit</span>::<span>Day</span><span>)</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>            </span><span>.</span><span>relative</span><span>(</span><span>"2022-01-01"</span><span>.</span><span>parse</span>::<span>&lt;</span><span>jiff</span>::<span>civil</span>::<span>Date</span><span>&gt;</span><span>()</span><span>?</span><span>),</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>)</span><span>?</span><span>;</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>assert_eq!</span><span>(</span><span>rounded_span</span><span>.</span><span>to_string</span><span>(),</span><span> </span><span>"P32D"</span><span>);</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>    </span><span>Ok</span><span>(())</span><span>
</span></span></span><span><span><span>}</span><span>
</span></span></span></code></pre></div><p>The main difference between these examples is that Jiff’s duration type is
named <code>Span</code> instead of <code>Duration</code>. This was done to avoid confusion with
Rust’s standard library <code>std::time::Duration</code> type.</p>
<p>Here are some other differences:</p>
<ul>
<li>Temporal’s <code>Instant</code> type in Jiff is named <code>Timestamp</code> in order to avoid
confusion with Rust’s standard library <code>std::time::Instant</code> type.</li>
<li>Temporal’s “plain” date and time types are called “civil” in Jiff.</li>
<li>Jiff only supports the Gregorian calendar.</li>
<li>Jiff’s datetime range only extends to +/- 9999, whereas Temporal’s range is
larger.</li>
</ul>
<p>It’s easy to say that without Temporal, Jiff wouldn’t exist in its current
form today.</p>
      </article>

      

    </div></div>]]></description>
