            <link>https://trmnl.com/blog/introducing-frame</link>
            <guid>50519354</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:23:22 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://trmnl.com/blog/introducing-frame">https://trmnl.com/blog/introducing-frame</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50519354">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-controller="blog-syntax-highlighter"><h3 id="tl-dr">tl;dr</h3><p>our 3rd release, a full color 31.5" ePaper display in a handmade wooden frame, is now available for pre-order. watch the short film:</p><figure><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/RmoSjWDiMEE?rel=0&amp;controls=0" title="YouTube video" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen"></iframe></figure><p>the 1st batch is just ~470 units and i intend to ship at least a few by the end of 2026. most deliveries will happen early 2027. see <a href="#logistics">logistics</a> for more details or <a href="https://shop.trmnl.com/products/frame">pre-order here</a>.</p><h3 id="background">background</h3><p>in 2013 i founded ArtSpot. our vision was to give New Yorkers a bit of joy by exposing them to local art. we did this through informal agreements with dozens of cafes, small galleries, and restaurants. "<em>let us curate art on your walls, get paid if someone buys it.</em>"</p><p>and it worked. sort of. finding artists was easy, building the platform was easy. getting permission from venues was slow but straightforward. the problem was nobody actually bought the art. i remember the admin email notification that first time a piece was sold. it was a small painting of Janelle Monáe. the buyer was my friend, a gift to me. here it is, still in my possession.</p><action-text-attachment sgid="eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6ImdpZDovL3RybW5sL0FjdGl2ZVN0b3JhZ2U6OkJsb2IvMTk4Mjk2OTc_ZXhwaXJlc19pbiIsInB1ciI6ImF0dGFjaGFibGUifX0=--15708c5e95a4a1a4598f4ae2a1055ada36da6848" content-type="image/png" url="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/blobs/redirect/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4Mjk2OTcsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--d22cbeb9b5c541497648a8c992baba87797fadf7/janelle-front-back.png" filename="janelle-front-back.png" filesize="1329146" width="695" height="436" previewable="true" presentation="gallery" caption="">  <figure>
      <img data-image-lightbox-src="/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4Mjk2OTcsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--d22cbeb9b5c541497648a8c992baba87797fadf7/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJwbmciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsyNTYwLDI1NjBdfSwicHVyIjoidmFyaWF0aW9uIn19--916f83e17a3f8c707d48735747fadce268546db3/janelle-front-back.png" src="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4Mjk2OTcsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--d22cbeb9b5c541497648a8c992baba87797fadf7/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJwbmciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsxMDI0LDc2OF19LCJwdXIiOiJ2YXJpYXRpb24ifX0=--a1b29f018116e3f4abd0440beb02228a54f47dd1/janelle-front-back.png">

  </figure></action-text-attachment><p>anyway ArtSpot failed. five years later i paid back my investors (+ 13% interest). but the vision never went away. i love making and consuming art.</p><h3 id="opinionated-platform">opinionated platform</h3><p>i probably say "<em>the most popular feature request since launch is ______</em>" too often. after saying no (and yes) to so many requests, it's tough to keep count.</p><p>but with certainty, one of our strongest convictions was about color. TRMNL is not a television. it is not a clock. we avoid trying to do too much.</p><p>then <a href="https://trmnl.com/blog/no-more-flicker">grayscale happened</a>. photos look great. art is possible. community member Clark debuted <em>Jaws</em> (yes the movie), <a href="https://x.com/clarklab/status/1921209108377788721">1 frame at a time</a>.</p><action-text-attachment sgid="eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6ImdpZDovL3RybW5sL0FjdGl2ZVN0b3JhZ2U6OkJsb2IvMTk4Mjk4MDQ_ZXhwaXJlc19pbiIsInB1ciI6ImF0dGFjaGFibGUifX0=--3b08de10ad23ab69271186201ed094ec3d97123f" content-type="image/png" url="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/blobs/redirect/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4Mjk4MDQsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--5e58d212fcdb23115dadb790b3df805e545f380a/image.png" filename="image.png" filesize="88297" width="695" height="417" previewable="true" presentation="gallery" caption="1-bit Jaws on TRMNL">  <figure>
      <img data-image-lightbox-src="/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4Mjk4MDQsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--5e58d212fcdb23115dadb790b3df805e545f380a/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJwbmciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsyNTYwLDI1NjBdfSwicHVyIjoidmFyaWF0aW9uIn19--916f83e17a3f8c707d48735747fadce268546db3/image.png" src="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4Mjk4MDQsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--5e58d212fcdb23115dadb790b3df805e545f380a/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJwbmciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsxMDI0LDc2OF19LCJwdXIiOiJ2YXJpYXRpb24ifX0=--a1b29f018116e3f4abd0440beb02228a54f47dd1/image.png">

      <figcaption>
          1-bit Jaws on TRMNL
      </figcaption>
  </figure></action-text-attachment><p>which got us thinking... maybe we were wrong about color. maybe it aligns with our vision and just maybe, it makes it even bigger.</p><h3 id="introducing-frame">introducing Frame</h3><p>today i'm thrilled to bury the lede and announce <a href="https://shop.trmnl.com/products/frame">Frame</a>. it's a 31.5 inch full color ePaper display, enclosed in a hand made wooden body that we manufacture ourselves. yes, we opened our own woodworking shop a few miles from HQ. a closer look at the workshop is coming soon.</p><action-text-attachment sgid="eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6ImdpZDovL3RybW5sL0FjdGl2ZVN0b3JhZ2U6OkJsb2IvMTk4MzE2Mzk_ZXhwaXJlc19pbiIsInB1ciI6ImF0dGFjaGFibGUifX0=--2a0a92b711eec465175c6c9ff3ab0fc8980c4750" content-type="image/jpeg" url="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/blobs/redirect/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzE2MzksInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--0043b9a63057a343579da0458a8efdeea5ad024c/trmnl-frame-on-kickstand.jpg" filename="trmnl-frame-on-kickstand.jpg" filesize="221450" width="695" height="391" previewable="true" presentation="gallery" caption="Schedule + Weather + Art in 1 screen">  <figure>
      <img data-image-lightbox-src="/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzE2MzksInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--0043b9a63057a343579da0458a8efdeea5ad024c/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsyNTYwLDI1NjBdfSwicHVyIjoidmFyaWF0aW9uIn19--dca01e04df3bbc72f37a667e31be1efca2488daa/trmnl-frame-on-kickstand.jpg" src="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzE2MzksInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--0043b9a63057a343579da0458a8efdeea5ad024c/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsxMDI0LDc2OF19LCJwdXIiOiJ2YXJpYXRpb24ifX0=--d5e667c0137ab726f37f5f3b8ccaf980246e425b/trmnl-frame-on-kickstand.jpg">

      <figcaption>
          Schedule + Weather + Art in 1 screen
      </figcaption>
  </figure></action-text-attachment><p>Frame gets all the same features as other TRMNL devices, with a few additional perks spanning both the web platform and the hardware itself. <a href="https://youtu.be/RmoSjWDiMEE">watch the short film</a> or read along below.</p><p><strong>Clock Sync</strong></p><p>often requested, always ignored, until now. Frame will update at specified moments of the day versus a relative "every N minutes" cadence. this reduces screen flicker while also extending the battery life and adding continuity to your schedule.</p><action-text-attachment sgid="eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6ImdpZDovL3RybW5sL0FjdGl2ZVN0b3JhZ2U6OkJsb2IvMTk4Mjk4NDY_ZXhwaXJlc19pbiIsInB1ciI6ImF0dGFjaGFibGUifX0=--67a9cf01c4428de023164cde834082cf38a6354a" content-type="image/png" url="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/blobs/redirect/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4Mjk4NDYsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--6ff2fbc655849fe1250ae91bf918c179bce90f0b/trmnl-clock-sync.png" filename="trmnl-clock-sync.png" filesize="119159" width="490" height="512" previewable="true" presentation="gallery" caption="">  <figure>
      <img data-image-lightbox-src="/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4Mjk4NDYsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--6ff2fbc655849fe1250ae91bf918c179bce90f0b/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJwbmciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsyNTYwLDI1NjBdfSwicHVyIjoidmFyaWF0aW9uIn19--916f83e17a3f8c707d48735747fadce268546db3/trmnl-clock-sync.png" src="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4Mjk4NDYsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--6ff2fbc655849fe1250ae91bf918c179bce90f0b/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJwbmciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsxMDI0LDc2OF19LCJwdXIiOiJ2YXJpYXRpb24ifX0=--a1b29f018116e3f4abd0440beb02228a54f47dd1/trmnl-clock-sync.png">

  </figure></action-text-attachment><p><strong>Offline Mode</strong></p><action-text-attachment sgid="eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6ImdpZDovL3RybW5sL0FjdGl2ZVN0b3JhZ2U6OkJsb2IvMTk4MzA1NTU_ZXhwaXJlc19pbiIsInB1ciI6ImF0dGFjaGFibGUifX0=--e8d595dd2deec41a2ad30cc83e94073a1f386015" content-type="image/jpeg" url="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/blobs/redirect/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzA1NTUsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--4aafffa109faf77b4a2dc8409acca63562a3e848/trmnl-frame-offline-mode.jpg" filename="trmnl-frame-offline-mode.jpg" filesize="219100" width="695" height="281" previewable="true" presentation="gallery" caption="the best interface is no interface">  <figure>
      <img data-image-lightbox-src="/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzA1NTUsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--4aafffa109faf77b4a2dc8409acca63562a3e848/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsyNTYwLDI1NjBdfSwicHVyIjoidmFyaWF0aW9uIn19--dca01e04df3bbc72f37a667e31be1efca2488daa/trmnl-frame-offline-mode.jpg" src="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzA1NTUsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--4aafffa109faf77b4a2dc8409acca63562a3e848/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsxMDI0LDc2OF19LCJwdXIiOiJ2YXJpYXRpb24ifX0=--d5e667c0137ab726f37f5f3b8ccaf980246e425b/trmnl-frame-offline-mode.jpg">

      <figcaption>
          the best interface is no interface
      </figcaption>
  </figure></action-text-attachment><p>the internet is cool but totally unnecessary. Frame's microSD port lets you run devices completely offline with zero personal information reaching our servers.</p><p><strong>Dedicated Power (optional)</strong></p><action-text-attachment sgid="eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6ImdpZDovL3RybW5sL0FjdGl2ZVN0b3JhZ2U6OkJsb2IvMTk4MzA1NTM_ZXhwaXJlc19pbiIsInB1ciI6ImF0dGFjaGFibGUifX0=--0db47745407b9b4cf7e488a733cc97dd77ae572c" content-type="image/jpeg" url="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/blobs/redirect/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzA1NTMsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--1dc809aa3091e0ee7f8b70d796bf086aebb34bd1/trmnl-frame-back-usb-port.jpg" filename="trmnl-frame-back-usb-port.jpg" filesize="297807" width="695" height="437" previewable="true" presentation="gallery" caption="1st port is available on the side panel">  <figure>
      <img data-image-lightbox-src="/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzA1NTMsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--1dc809aa3091e0ee7f8b70d796bf086aebb34bd1/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsyNTYwLDI1NjBdfSwicHVyIjoidmFyaWF0aW9uIn19--dca01e04df3bbc72f37a667e31be1efca2488daa/trmnl-frame-back-usb-port.jpg" src="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzA1NTMsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--1dc809aa3091e0ee7f8b70d796bf086aebb34bd1/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsxMDI0LDc2OF19LCJwdXIiOiJ2YXJpYXRpb24ifX0=--d5e667c0137ab726f37f5f3b8ccaf980246e425b/trmnl-frame-back-usb-port.jpg">

      <figcaption>
          1st port is available on the side panel
      </figcaption>
  </figure></action-text-attachment><p>like all TRMNL devices, a rechargeable battery is included (10-20k mAh available). but with Frame we added an extra USB-C port on the rear of the device, accessible with a small hole through the back panel for always-on connectivity.</p><p><strong>Color Expertise</strong></p><action-text-attachment sgid="eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6ImdpZDovL3RybW5sL0FjdGl2ZVN0b3JhZ2U6OkJsb2IvMTk4MzAwNjQ_ZXhwaXJlc19pbiIsInB1ciI6ImF0dGFjaGFibGUifX0=--5721a6eda8317d77ffc168a5af1f8a782074f534" content-type="image/jpeg" url="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/blobs/redirect/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzAwNjQsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--a9188b20fe76b8daa335b3ed3fab7c4b4b194604/trmnl-lazarus-workbench.jpeg" filename="trmnl-lazarus-workbench.jpeg" filesize="177635" width="695" height="424" previewable="true" presentation="gallery" caption="Lazarus workbench">  <figure>
      <img data-image-lightbox-src="/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzAwNjQsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--a9188b20fe76b8daa335b3ed3fab7c4b4b194604/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGVnIiwicmVzaXplX3RvX2xpbWl0IjpbMjU2MCwyNTYwXX0sInB1ciI6InZhcmlhdGlvbiJ9fQ==--83a50e5021e7f6e19e2ee2fa117bfcf2b44b7743/trmnl-lazarus-workbench.jpeg" src="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzAwNjQsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--a9188b20fe76b8daa335b3ed3fab7c4b4b194604/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGVnIiwicmVzaXplX3RvX2xpbWl0IjpbMTAyNCw3NjhdfSwicHVyIjoidmFyaWF0aW9uIn19--b5fc5af83c336b0fe3eb199eed01a2c85b3a702d/trmnl-lazarus-workbench.jpeg">

      <figcaption>
          Lazarus workbench
      </figcaption>
  </figure></action-text-attachment><p>some of my colleagues are snobs about ePaper dithering algorithms and LUTs (look-up tables). this is now your gain.</p><p>over the past few months we've been developing Lazarus, a color conversion engine that we think provides Frame the best color matching on the market for any Spectra 6 display. it's also coming soon to our <a href="https://shop.trmnl.com/products/trmnl-bwry">4-color device</a>, and yes it will be open source for your own tuning pleasure.</p><p><strong>Three Orientations</strong></p><action-text-attachment sgid="eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6ImdpZDovL3RybW5sL0FjdGl2ZVN0b3JhZ2U6OkJsb2IvMTk4MzE2NDM_ZXhwaXJlc19pbiIsInB1ciI6ImF0dGFjaGFibGUifX0=--f4165a8503f8e1e5a72ec68fad1d68c611a59041" content-type="image/jpeg" url="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/blobs/redirect/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzE2NDMsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--7308f7908549b079ad24df6b6216c8bc4e4fc17c/trmnl-frame-portrait-example.jpg" filename="trmnl-frame-portrait-example.jpg" filesize="465377" width="695" height="391" previewable="true" presentation="gallery" caption="Frame at Cafe Brûlée in LaGrange, Georgia">  <figure>
      <img data-image-lightbox-src="/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzE2NDMsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--7308f7908549b079ad24df6b6216c8bc4e4fc17c/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsyNTYwLDI1NjBdfSwicHVyIjoidmFyaWF0aW9uIn19--dca01e04df3bbc72f37a667e31be1efca2488daa/trmnl-frame-portrait-example.jpg" src="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzE2NDMsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--7308f7908549b079ad24df6b6216c8bc4e4fc17c/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJqcGciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsxMDI0LDc2OF19LCJwdXIiOiJ2YXJpYXRpb24ifX0=--d5e667c0137ab726f37f5f3b8ccaf980246e425b/trmnl-frame-portrait-example.jpg">

      <figcaption>
          Frame at Cafe Brûlée in LaGrange, Georgia
      </figcaption>
  </figure></action-text-attachment><p>Hang Frame in portrait or landscape orientation without additional hardware, both are built-in and flush mounted. You can optionally buy or build your own kickstand.</p><p><strong>And More</strong></p><action-text-attachment sgid="eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6ImdpZDovL3RybW5sL0FjdGl2ZVN0b3JhZ2U6OkJsb2IvMTk4MzAwOTA_ZXhwaXJlc19pbiIsInB1ciI6ImF0dGFjaGFibGUifX0=--8ac0212e6a5fa5483b4f6e453faacf9258089236" content-type="image/png" url="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/blobs/redirect/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzAwOTAsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--77d32e6913d54b6b5a3740096b7b7d7ae479f189/trmnl-frame-local-art-plugin-example.png" filename="trmnl-frame-local-art-plugin-example.png" filesize="126236" width="695" height="410" previewable="true" presentation="gallery" caption="">  <figure>
      <img data-image-lightbox-src="/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzAwOTAsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--77d32e6913d54b6b5a3740096b7b7d7ae479f189/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJwbmciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsyNTYwLDI1NjBdfSwicHVyIjoidmFyaWF0aW9uIn19--916f83e17a3f8c707d48735747fadce268546db3/trmnl-frame-local-art-plugin-example.png" src="https://trmnl.com/rails/active_storage/representations/proxy/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6MTk4MzAwOTAsInB1ciI6ImJsb2JfaWQifX0=--77d32e6913d54b6b5a3740096b7b7d7ae479f189/eyJfcmFpbHMiOnsiZGF0YSI6eyJmb3JtYXQiOiJwbmciLCJyZXNpemVfdG9fbGltaXQiOlsxMDI0LDc2OF19LCJwdXIiOiJ2YXJpYXRpb24ifX0=--a1b29f018116e3f4abd0440beb02228a54f47dd1/trmnl-frame-local-art-plugin-example.png">

  </figure></action-text-attachment><p>we're negotiating with some of our favorite community developers to build more Frame-centric features. paying homage to <a href="#background">ArtSpot</a>, we just created a Local Art plugin where anyone can <a href="https://trmnl.com/local-art/submit">submit their work</a> and even sell it through the TRMNL platform.</p><h3 id="transparent-supply-chain">transparent supply chain</h3><p>before TRMNL i spent 10 years in b2b software and knew nothing about making physical products for everyday people. nobody ever blasted me on reddit about injection mold flow lines or damaged UPS packages. at best i'd get a G2 review every 3 months.</p><p>this all changed in <a href="https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/usetrmnl/trmnl-the-e-ink-display-for-your-favorite-apps-and-news">July 2024</a> and after shipping 60,000 devices to 79 countries we've gotten pickier about operational design. chief among these new requirements is doing as much production as possible in-house.</p><p>here are Frame's components and where they're sourced:</p><figure><table><tbody><tr><th><p><strong>Component</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Location</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Lead Times</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Inventory</strong></p></th></tr><tr><th><p>EPD (31.5" screen)</p></th><td><p>Overseas</p></td><td><p>2-3 months</p></td><td><p>300 in warehouse, 200 en route</p></td></tr><tr><th><p>Packaging</p></th><td><p>Alabama, USA</p></td><td><p>1-2 months</p></td><td><p>samples received, design tweaks sent for larger batch</p></td></tr><tr><th><p>Wooden body</p></th><td><p>TRMNL shop, Pine Mountain, Georgia</p></td><td><p>1 week</p></td><td><p>made-to-order pending wood species chosen at checkout</p></td></tr><tr><th><p>USB-C Charging Cable and Block</p></th><td><p>Overseas</p></td><td><p>3-4 weeks</p></td><td><p>500 in warehouse, includes American + European style adapters</p></td></tr><tr><th><p>Plastic parts</p></th><td><p>TRMNL HQ, LaGrange, Georgia</p></td><td><p>1-2 days</p></td><td><p>made daily from a growing fleet of 3D printers</p></td></tr><tr><th><p>PCBA</p></th><td><p>Overseas</p></td><td><p>2-3 weeks</p></td><td><p>1,000 full assemblies (raw components) in warehouse, bulk pick &amp; place order TBD</p></td></tr><tr><th><p>Fasteners</p></th><td><p>Overseas + USA</p></td><td><p>2-3 weeks</p></td><td><p>500 full assemblies ordered, en route next week</p></td></tr><tr><th><p>Rigid foam</p></th><td><p>Overseas + USA</p></td><td><p>1 week</p></td><td><p>50 in warehouse, 450 en route</p></td></tr><tr><th><p>Batteries</p></th><td><p>Overseas</p></td><td><p>2-3 weeks</p></td><td><p>Samples in warehouse, 800 ordered, en route next week</p></td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p>by reducing complexity, <a href="https://trmnl.com/blog/on-x">learning from</a> past releases, and having everything we need either in stock or on order, i expect this to be our smoothest fulfillment cycle yet. as with other devices, all Frames are assembled by our own team and never outsourced.</p><h3 id="logistics">logistics</h3><p>Batch #1 is available to customers in locations our USA warehouse already ships to: USA, Canada, Mexico, Australia. <a href="https://shop.trmnl.com/products/frame">pre-order here</a>.</p><p>we're open to shipping elsewhere, but Duty / Import fees could be quite high given the product's Customs value. <strong>to participate in Batch #1 as a resident outside these countries</strong>, <a href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSc5oFPs4TqFvyo13ipocjO5n1eK545V58Q8_CwD8oVSXy0bRQ/viewform?usp=publish-editor"><strong>go here</strong></a>. this option may be de-activated without notice if inventory sells out.</p><p>in early 2027 we plan to open a 2nd woodworking shop near our Berlin, Germany warehouse to offer postage- and Duty-free fulfillment from a TRMNL team near you.</p>
</div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Jolt – Clojure that speaks C (102 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://jolt-lang.net/</link>
            <guid>50519345</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:23:01 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://jolt-lang.net/">https://jolt-lang.net/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50519345">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page">
    <nav>
      
    </nav>

    

    
<header>
  <div>
    <div>
      <h2>Clojure that speaks C</h2>
      <p>
        Jolt is a self-hosted Clojure implementation that runs on
        a portable Scheme layer that supports multiple backends.
        Chez natively, Gambit for JavaScript, no JVM. Most Clojure
        runs unchanged, any C library binds natively, and <code>jolt build</code>
        ships a single standalone binary.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Native C interop: <code>defcfn</code> turns C functions into ordinary Clojure fns</li>
        <li><code>jolt build</code> emits a single standalone binary with C dependencies linked in statically</li>
        <li>Persistent data structures, lazy seqs, transducers, multimethods, and protocols with full Clojure semantics</li>
        <li>Real concurrency: <code>future</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>pmap</code>, and <code>core.async</code> on OS threads</li>
      </ul>
      
      <p>The terminal on the right is jolt itself, compiled to
        JavaScript by the <a href="https://jolt-lang.net/docs/scheme-backends.html">Gambit backend</a>,
        evaluating live in your browser. It runs a
        <a href="https://jolt-lang.net/docs/scheme-backends.html#choosing_how_much_of_the_language_to_build">reduced
        build</a> to keep the download small, so regex is left out here and says
        so if you reach for it.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p><span></span><span></span><span></span>
        <span id="jolt-repl-status">jolt via Gambit → JS</span>
        <span>repl</span>
      </p>
      <div id="jolt-repl-out"><p>user=&gt; (-&gt;&gt; (range 10)
         (filter even?)
         (map #(* % %))
         (reduce +))</p><p>120</p></div>
      
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<div id="features">
    <h2>Why Jolt?</h2>
    <div>
      <div>
        
        <h4>Native C FFI</h4>
        <p>Bind any C shared library: declare it, wrap functions with <code>defcfn</code>, call them with Clojure values. The db library reaches SQLite and Postgres this way; http-client binds OpenSSL and zlib.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        
        <h4>Standalone Binaries</h4>
        <p><code>jolt build</code> ahead-of-time compiles a project (runtime, standard library, app, and its deps) into a single self-contained executable, with C dependencies linked statically. No Chez, no JVM, no source needed to run it.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        
        <h4>Self-Hosted Compiler</h4>
        <p>Reads Clojure, analyzes it to a host-neutral IR, emits Scheme, and runs it: on Chez natively, or on Gambit compiled to JavaScript. The compiler is written in Clojure and compiles itself; Chez runs interpreted and compiled code identically, so the REPL and a built binary behave the same.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        
        <h4>Real Concurrency</h4>
        <p><code>future</code>/<code>promise</code>/<code>agent</code>/<code>pmap</code> run on OS threads over a shared heap, matching JVM semantics. <code>core.async</code> provides channels and <code>go</code> blocks.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        
        <h4>Persistent Data Structures</h4>
        <p>Immutable vectors (32-way tries), cons lists, and HAMT maps/sets with Clojure value semantics. Transients are real mutable scratch collections.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        
        <h4>Clojure-Compatible</h4>
        <p>Lazy/infinite seqs, transducers, destructuring, multimethods, protocols/records, metadata, namespaces, runtime <code>eval</code>, and the full reader.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

<div id="quickstart">
    <h2>Quick Start</h2>
    <p>Install the self-contained <code>jolt</code> binary: it bundles the runtime, compiler, and standard library, so there's nothing else to install.</p>
    <pre><code><span># Homebrew</span>
brew install jolt-lang/jolt/jolt

<span># or the install script (Linux / macOS)</span>
curl -sL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jolt-lang/jolt/main/install | bash

jolt -e <span>'(+ 1 2)'</span>        <span># =&gt; 3</span></code></pre>
    <p>Or run from a clone (needs <a href="https://cisco.github.io/ChezScheme/">Chez Scheme</a>); the bootstrap seed is checked in, so there is no build step:</p>
    <pre><code>git clone --recurse-submodules https://github.com/jolt-lang/jolt.git
<span>cd</span> jolt
jolt -e <span>'(+ 1 2)'</span>        <span># =&gt; 3</span></code></pre>
  </div>

<div id="usage">
    <h2>Usage</h2>

    <h3>Evaluate an expression</h3>
    <pre><code>$ jolt -e <span>'(-&gt;&gt; (range 10) (filter even?) (map (fn [x] (* x x))) (reduce +))'</span>
<span># 120</span>
$ jolt -e <span>'(/ 1 2)'</span>
<span># 1/2</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Run a project</h3>
    <pre><code>jolt run -m myapp.core   <span># resolve deps.edn, then run -main</span>
jolt -M:test [args]      <span># run an alias's :main-opts</span>
jolt path                <span># print the resolved source roots</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Compile a standalone binary</h3>
    <pre><code>jolt build -m myapp.core -o myapp   <span># single self-contained executable</span>
./myapp arg1 arg2                        <span># runs anywhere; args reach -main</span>
<span># --opt for the optimized build, --dev for an unoptimized one</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Bind a C library</h3>
    <pre><code>(require '[jolt.ffi :as ffi])
(ffi/load-library <span>"libsqlite3.dylib"</span>)  <span>; or declare :jolt/native in deps.edn</span>

(ffi/defcfn sqlite3-libversion <span>"sqlite3_libversion"</span> [] :string)
(sqlite3-libversion)             <span>; =&gt; "3.51.0"</span></code></pre>
    <p>Any C library binds the same way: the <a href="https://jolt-lang.net/docs/native-interop.html">native interop guide</a> covers types, memory, structs, and static linking.</p>

    <h3>nREPL</h3>
    <pre><code>jolt --nrepl-server      <span># auto-resolves deps.edn, writes .nrepl-port</span>
<span># connect CIDER / Calva / Cursive via the .nrepl-port file, then</span>
<span># redefine a var and the next call sees it; no restart</span></code></pre>
    <p>Develop against a live process: see <a href="https://jolt-lang.net/docs/repl-driven-development.html">REPL-Driven Development</a>.</p>

    <p>Read the <a href="https://jolt-lang.net/docs/getting-started.html">documentation</a> for installation, writing libraries, and C interop.</p>
  </div>

<div id="differences">
    <h2>Differences from Clojure</h2>
    <p>Jolt targets Clojure semantics but runs on Scheme, not the JVM. Most portable Clojure runs unchanged: persistent collections, the numeric tower, lazy seqs, transducers, multimethods, protocols/records, atoms, <code>future</code>/<code>agent</code>/<code>pmap</code>, <code>core.async</code>, runtime <code>eval</code>, and the full reader all behave as on the JVM. The genuine divergences:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Difference</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Java interop</td><td>No JVM, so no general Java interop, reflection, or <code>gen-class</code>/<code>proxy</code>; a native C <a href="https://jolt-lang.net/docs/native-interop.html">FFI</a> takes its place, plus a shimmed subset of <code>java.*</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>BigDecimal</td><td>Not available (<code>decimal?</code> is always false); the rest of the numeric tower matches</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Regex</td><td>Compiled by <a href="https://github.com/ashinn/irregex" target="_blank" rel="noopener">irregex</a>, not <code>java.util.regex</code>; common patterns work, some Java-specific features differ</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>


    

    
    
    
    
    


  

</div>]]></description>
